    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---DNA-Aptamer-Chemist_JR26132": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - DNA/Aptamer Chemist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow - DNA/Aptamer Chemist Department Research | Perrin Lab | Department of Chemistry | Faculty of Science (David Perrin) Posting End Date October 23, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date October 31, 2027     The expected pay range for this position is $55,000 - $65,000/annually. University of British Columbia, Perrin Laboratory  (https://perrin.chem.ubc.ca/) We are seeking a highly motivated DNA/Aptamer Chemist to join a multidisciplinary research team developing nucleic acid-based targeting agents to treat prostate cancer. The successful candidate will focus on the design, synthesis, modification and biophysical characterization of DNA aptamers. This position would be particularly well suited to a chemist or chemical biologist interested in applying nucleic acid chemistry to translational cancer research. Responsibilities ●       Design and synthesis of DNA aptamers and modified oligonucleotides. ●       Solid-phase oligonucleotide synthesis, purification and quality control. ●       Chemical modification and functionalization of DNA, including incorporation of linkers, fluorophores, chelators and other functional groups. ●       Purification and analytical characterization using HPLC, LC-MS and related approaches. ●       Systematic structure-function studies using nucleotide substitutions, truncations and chemical modifications. ●       Development of aptamers with improved affinity, selectivity and stability. ●       Quantitative characterization of aptamer-protein interactions using biochemical and biophysical approaches. ●       Conjugation of aptamers to imaging or therapeutic payloads. ●       Collaboration with protein biochemists, molecular biologists, structural biologists and imaging scientists. Qualifications Candidates should have a PhD in chemistry, chemical biology, biochemistry or a closely related field. Candidates must have demonstrated experience i.e. published work in several of the following areas: ●       Oligonucleotide/DNA chemistry ●       Solid-phase oligonucleotide synthesis ●       Nucleic acid modification and conjugation ●       Aptamer development or SELEX ●       Protein-ligand binding assays ●       Bioanalytic skill including techniques such as SPR, BLI, MST, ITC, HPLC and LC-MS ●       Structure-function analysis of nucleic acids ●       Nucleic acid-protein interactions Work Location:   ·        The primary work location is the UBC Vancouver Campus Application Process: ·        Seeking full-time PDF for 12 months with the possibility of extension. Deadline to Apply:  Applications will be reviewed so long as the job posting is live.   Experience with radiochemistry, chelator chemistry, structural biology or therapeutic oligonucleotide development would be an asset but is not required.   We are looking for someone with strong experimental skills who can independently design and troubleshoot experiments, develop new chemical and biochemical approaches, and work effectively within a collaborative multidisciplinary research environment. We kindly request that you not send follow-up emails inquiring as to the status of your application. Applicants who do not have demonstrated skills in the above skill set, we may not take further action on your application. Successful candidates will be retained for an interview by zoom/teams. Please submit the following documents in your application: A CV Cover Letter Please indicate your legal status to work in Canada. Equity and Diversity Statement UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. Inclusion is built by individual and institutional responsibility through continuous engagement with diversity to inspire people, ideas, and actions for a better world. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Métis, Inuk, or Indigenous person. UBC acknowledges that certain circumstances may cause career interruptions that legitimately affect an applicant’s record of research or educational leadership achievement. We encourage applicants to note in their applications whether they would like consideration given to the impact of any circumstances, such as those due to health or family reasons, in order to allow for a fair assessment of their research productivity. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority, and members of historically marginalized groups will be given special consideration. If you have any needs or questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the job application, recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Center For Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/working-injury-illness-ordisability/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca ."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Research-Associate_JR26176-1": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Research Associate Department Cembrowski Laboratory | Department of Cellular and Physiological Sciences | Faculty of Medicine (Mark Steven Cembrowski) Posting End Date October 30, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date December 31, 2027     The expected salary for this position is $84,872.04. The Department of Cellular & Physiological Sciences at the University of British Columbia invites applications for a full-time Research Associate to join the laboratory of Dr. Mark Cembrowski. This is a six-month term position with a start date of January 1, 2027, and an end date of June 30, 2027. The Department of Cellular and Physiological Sciences does world-class research aimed at understanding the fundamental workings of cells and organisms in an effort to identify the underlying causes of human diseases. The Department’s research is diverse, with Investigators working in a wide range of the leading invertebrate and vertebrate model systems and using biochemical, molecular genetic, cellular and physiological approaches. The Department has particular strengths in neuroscience, endocrinology, development and cancer. The robust collaborative spirit between the Department’s Investigators drives increasingly multidisciplinary research programs in each laboratory. This includes the incorporation of the latest technologies in molecular biology, genetics, next-generation genomics, proteomics, bioinformatics, and genetically-engineered animal models to complement our expertise in cell biological and physiology. The research in the Department is also strengthened by our use of advanced imaging capabilities that includes EM, super-resolution, 2-photon, live-cell and high-throughput microscopy. Research in the Department of Cellular and Physiological Sciences is providing insight into the fundamental biological processes and pathologies that lie behind many diseases, including cancer, Alzheimer’s, diabetes, heart disease, and stroke, to name just a few.   Responsibilities will include but are not limited to the following:   Reporting to Mark Cembrowski, Principal Investigator and Associate Professor in the Department of Cellular & Physiological Sciences, the successful candidate will contribute to both independent and collaborative research projects. Responsibilities will include:   ·         Electrophysiological recording of brain tissue      ·         Viral circuit mapping of brain tissue      ·         Viral manipulation of brain tissue      ·         Histological processing and microscopic analysis of brain tissue      ·         Interfacing with Vancouver General Hospital surgical teams as needed to foster successful resource sharing and collaboration with Cembrowski laboratory      ·         Interfacing with members of the Cembrowski laboratory as needed to foster successful research, including coordinating receipt of materials from Vancouver General Hospital   ·         Monitoring use of reagents and other supplies, and order supplies when needed      ·         Disseminating scientific findings by writing and publishing manuscripts and grant applications  ·         Maintaining complete and accurate records of all work performed      ·         Training students and technicians as needed    ·         Perform other related duties as required.   The successful candidate should have the following:   Ph.D. and postdoctoral work in neuroscience or a related discipline. Demonstrated record of scientific productivity, including peer-reviewed publications and evidence of independent research contributions. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a collaborative and multidisciplinary research environment. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and scientific communication skills. The drive, initiative, and enthusiasm to build and complete a successful research project. Ability to work in a fast-paced and demanding team environment. Has the drive and enthusiasm to lead and work as a member of a team.   Candidates interested must apply via the UBC Careers website .   UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities   As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world.   UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Hospital-Site---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR26054": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department Eddy Laboratory | Department of Radiology | Faculty of Medicine (Rachel Eddy) Posting End Date October 23, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. This position is subject to the satisfactory completion of required background checks Job End Date December 31, 2027     The expected pay range for this position is $5,416.67 – $6,666.67/month. At UBC, we believe that attracting and sustaining a diverse workforce is key to the successful pursuit of excellence in research, innovation, and learning for all faculty, staff and students. Our commitment to employment equity helps achieve inclusion and fairness, brings rich diversity to UBC as a workplace, and creates the necessary conditions for a rewarding career. The Eddy Lab, Department of Radiology, Faculty of Medicine is seeking a full-time Postdoctoral Fellow to develop novel 129Xe magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) acquisition techniques for pulmonary imaging. The project is funded through an NSERC Discovery Grant, and the PDF salary is supported through an approved Mitacs Accelerate internship with partner Siemens Healthcare Limited. This is an on-site position located at the UBC Centre for Heart Lung Innovation (HLI), St. Paul’s Hospital. The UBC Faculty of Medicine Department of Radiology is the home department for this Postdoctoral Fellow position. HLI is a UBC senate-approved Centre located within Providence Health Care’s St. Paul’s Hospital in downtown Vancouver. HLI’s MRI Core was established in June 2021, with a focus on advanced imaging of patients with heart and lung disease. The facility houses a state-of-the-art 3.0T clinical MRI scanner and hyperpolarized 129Xe gas facility for functional imaging of the lungs. HLI is one of two centers in Vancouver and only five centers in Canada with hyperpolarized 129Xe MRI capabilities. To date, the MRI Core operates numerous ongoing clinical studies in pulmonary imaging and has amassed a pulmonary imaging registry including images from more than 500 research participants. The PDF will report directly to the Principal Investigator, Dr. Rachel Eddy. The successful candidate with liaise with Siemens Healthcare Limited partner personnel, as well as a multidisciplinary team of imaging scientists, engineers, MRI technologists, physicians, undergraduate and graduate trainees, and research technicians and coordinators. Responsibilities Reporting to the Principal Investigator, Dr. Rachel Eddy, the incumbent will be responsible for: Development and optimization of rapid pulse sequences for 129Xe MRI Development of image reconstruction techniques for novel 129Xe pulse sequences Supervising undergraduate and junior graduate students, and technicians in the lab Developing, writing, and editing funding proposals for operating grants and fellowships In collaboration with the supervisor, writing research projects for publication Delivering scientific presentations at local, national, and/or international conferences Contributing to a respectful, inclusive working environment Conducting literature reviews and synthesizing evidence into current-state synopses to identify knowledge gaps Developing research protocols and leading original research projects as required Liaising with Siemens Healthcare Limited partner personnel and a multidisciplinary team of imaging scientists, engineers, MRI technologists, physicians, trainees, and research staff Performing other responsibilities, as required Qualifications Successful applicants will have: A PhD in engineering, physics, or related discipline (completed within the last 5 years, with provision for career gaps) Experience developing and implementing novel MRI pulse sequences; experience with multi-nuclear pulse sequence development and the Siemens MR IDEA Sequence Development Environment (SDE) are considered assets Experience developing and performing novel MR image reconstruction techniques; experience with multi-nuclear image reconstruction, Siemens MR IDEA Image Calculation Environment (ICE), Python and MATLAB, and deep learning techniques are considered assets A strong publication record demonstrating research excellence. A minimum of one first-author publication is required. Excellent written and verbal communication skills The ability to work independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary team Excellent organizational skills, along with an outstanding level of accuracy and attention to detail Ability to exercise tact, discretion, and confidentiality and to contribute to a respectful, cooperative, and supportive work environment Ability to multi-task and prioritize work to meet deadlines Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own. Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion. The expected salary for this position is $65,000-$80,000 per annum. The Faculty of Medicine is committed to offering equitable and competitive salaries, commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. Application Procedure An application package should include: A letter of application outlining the applicant’s interest in the research and the position; A detailed curriculum vitae; Contact information for three referees Applications should be submitted online via UBC Workday Careers. Should you have any queries around this position, please contact Dr. Rachel Eddy at Rachel.Eddy@hli.ubc.ca .   Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date for this position is January 1, 2027 or upon a date to be mutually agreed. The initial term is one year, with the possibility for annual renewal. We invite applications from qualified candidates who share our commitment to employment equity and inclusive excellence, and we welcome applications from candidates belonging to historically, persistently, or systemically marginalized groups: Indigenous (First Nation, Métis, Inuit) Peoples, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women, 2SLGBTQIA+ people, and trans and non-binary people. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are appreciated, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we are committed to creating an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities).  Confidential accommodations are available on request. Please contact [Name] via email at [email]. If you have any questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/workplace-accessibility/centre-workplace-accessibility   or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca . With gratitude, we acknowledge that the University of British Columbia Faculty of Medicine and its distributed programs, which include four university academic campuses, are located on traditional, ancestral and unceded territories of First Nations Peoples and communities around the province. Our Vision: To Transform Health for Everyone. Ranked among the world’s top medical schools with the fifth-largest MD enrollment in North America, the UBC Faculty of Medicine is a leader in both the science and the practice of medicine. Across British Columbia, more than 12,000 faculty and staff are training the next generation of doctors and health care professionals, making remarkable discoveries, and helping to create the pathways to better health for our communities at home and around the world. The Faculty - comprised of approximately 2,200 administrative support, technical/research and management and professional staff, as well approximately 650 full-time academic and over 10,000 clinical faculty members - is composed of 19 academic basic science and/or clinical departments, three schools, and 24 research centres and institutes. Together with its University and Health Authority partners, the Faculty delivers innovative programs and conducts research in the areas of health and life sciences. Faculty, staff and trainees are located at university campuses, clinical academic campuses in hospital settings and other regionally based centres across the province. UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities. As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world. UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. med.ubc.ca | https://radiology.med.ubc.ca/"
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Hospital-Site---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR25981": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department Salker Laboratory | Department of Obstetrics & Gynaecology | Faculty of Medicine (Mady Salker) Posting End Date October 22, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. This position is subject to the satisfactory completion of required background checks Job End Date April 30, 2027     The expected salary for this position is $55,000 to $60,000/annum. At UBC, we believe that attracting and sustaining a diverse workforce is key to the successful pursuit of excellence in research, innovation, and learning for all faculty, staff and students. Our commitment to employment equity helps achieve inclusion and fairness, brings rich diversity to UBC as a workplace, and creates the necessary conditions for a rewarding career. ​​​​​​   We are seeking a highly motivated full-time (1.0 FTE) Postdoctoral Research Fellow (Bioinformatician) to join the  Salker research group, an innovative multidisciplinary initiative focused on improving maternal and infant health through advanced biomarkers, multi-omics, and translational research. This is a  6 months full time position  with a competitive salary, comprehensive health benefits, conference travel support, and the potential for extension subject to external funding. The successful candidate will join a collaborative research environment within the  University of British Columbia ,  BC Women's Hospital + Health Centre , and the  BC Children's Hospital Research Institute , working closely with clinicians, scientists, bioinformaticians, and trainees across obstetrics, neonatology, genomics, immunology, and computational biology. This position offers an exceptional opportunity for individuals interested in pursuing careers in medicine, computational biology, translational research, maternal-fetal medicine, reproductive biology, genomics, or precision health. Upon appointment, the Fellow will have access to well-characterized clinical cohorts and existing multi-omics datasets for analysis and publication. They will also contribute to the development of new prospective clinical studies, including the establishment of standardized clinical databases and biospecimen collection pipelines across maternal and neonatal cohorts. The Fellow will be encouraged to develop independent research questions aligned with the program and lead high-impact publications and grant applications. the Fellow may contribute to projects involving: multi-omics analyses (single-cell, cell-free RNA/DNA, methylation, proteomics); biomarker discovery for pregnancy complications and neonatal outcomes; computational biology, machine learning, and predictive modelling; translational studies integrating laboratory discoveries with clinical implementation. Vancouver is a vibrant coastal city located on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territories of the Coast Salish peoples. The city offers stunning natural beauty with easy access to the Pacific Ocean, Coast Mountains, and world-class outdoor recreation. Known for its diverse population, rich arts and food culture, and progressive values, Vancouver consistently ranks as one of the most livable cities in the world RESPONSIBILITIES Reporting to  Dr. Mady Salker , the Fellow will: Advance patient-oriented and clinic-based research which includes dissemination of existing data (i.e., publications and presentations) as well as launching new research. Develop and maintain bioinformatics pipelines for bulk and single-cell RNA sequencing, cell-free RNA, DNA methylation, proteomics, and other multi-omics datasets. Integrate diverse molecular and clinical datasets to identify biomarkers associated with adverse pregnancy and neonatal outcomes. Apply statistical modelling, machine learning, network analysis, and systems biology approaches to large-scale datasets. Perform longitudinal and multi-modal data integration to investigate disease trajectories and biological mechanisms. Collaborate with the multidisciplinary team at BCCHR and WHRI and mentor research trainees (e.g., undergraduate, graduate, postgraduate students). Participate in grant applications, including concept development, analysis of preliminary data, preparing background information, and writing grant sections. Apply for postdoctoral funding from federal, provincial, and other relevant agencies. QUALIFICATIONS Successful applicants will have: A doctoral degree (PhD or equivalent) in computational biology, biomedicine, women’s health research, or related fields. Substantial Experience in R, python or other relevant computational languages. Experience with single-cell RNA sequencing and/or ATAC-seq. Demonstrated experience developing and maintaining reproducible bioinformatics pipelines for high-dimensional or multi-omics datasets. Experience with statistical modelling, machine learning, and integration of molecular and clinical datasets. A strong or growing publication record as first author. Excellent organization, ethics/record keeping, time management, and communication skills. Excellent interpersonal skills that support teamwork. Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own. Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion. The expected salary for this position is $55,000 to $60,000. Dr. Salker will cover conference registration, travel, and shared accommodations for at least 1 North American conference, if presenting first author research. The Faculty of Medicine is committed to offering equitable and competitive salaries, commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. APPLICATION PROCEDURE An application package should include: Cover letter describing (a) relevant experience in bioinformatics, computational biology, and/or multi-omics research, (b) personal research interests, (c) reasons for pursuing a postdoctoral fellowship with the Salker research group, and (d) interest in a multi-year fellowship, pending external funding (2-page maximum); A detailed curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications and conference presentations; Names and contact information for 2-3 referees who will be able to provide a letter of reference within 5 business days upon request. Applications should be submitted online at https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs  Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please apply as soon as possible to facilitate timely review of your application. The anticipated start date for this position is November 1, 2026 or on a mutually agreed upon date. We invite applications from qualified candidates who share our commitment to employment equity and inclusive excellence, and we welcome applications from candidates belonging to historically, persistently, or systemically marginalized groups: Indigenous (First Nation, Métis, Inuit) Peoples, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women, 2SLGBTQIA+ people, and trans and non-binary people. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are appreciated, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we are committed to creating an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities).  Confidential accommodations are available on request. Please contact Lygia Siqueira, Assistant HR Manager, via email at lygia.siqueira@ubc.ca. If you have any questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/workplace-accessibility/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca .    With gratitude, we acknowledge that the University of British Columbia Faculty of Medicine and its distributed programs, which include four university academic campuses, are located on traditional, ancestral and unceded territories of First Nations Peoples and communities around the province. Our Vision: To Transform Health for Everyone. Ranked among the world’s top medical schools with the fifth-largest MD enrollment in North America, the UBC Faculty of Medicine is a leader in both the science and the practice of medicine. Across British Columbia, more than 12,000 faculty and staff are training the next generation of doctors and health care professionals, making remarkable discoveries, and helping to create the pathways to better health for our communities at home and around the world. The Faculty - comprised of approximately 2,200 administrative support, technical/research and management and professional staff, as well approximately 650 full-time academic and over 10,000 clinical faculty members - is composed of 19 academic basic science and/or clinical departments, three schools, and 24 research centres and institutes. Together with its University and Health Authority partners, the Faculty delivers innovative programs and conducts research in the areas of health and life sciences. Faculty, staff and trainees are located at university campuses, clinical academic campuses in hospital settings and other regionally based centres across the province. UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities. As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world. UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Hospital-Site---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR25978": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department Salker Laboratory | Department of Obstetrics & Gynaecology | Faculty of Medicine (Mady Salker) Posting End Date October 29, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. This position is subject to the satisfactory completion of required background checks Job End Date October 31, 2027     The expected salary for this position is $55,000 to $60,000/annum, plus benefits At UBC, we believe that attracting and sustaining a diverse workforce is key to the successful pursuit of excellence in research, innovation, and learning for all faculty, staff and students. Our commitment to employment equity helps achieve inclusion and fairness, brings rich diversity to UBC as a workplace, and creates the necessary conditions for a rewarding career. ​​​​​​   We are seeking a highly motivated full-time (1.0 FTE) Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the  Salker- research group, an innovative multidisciplinary initiative focused on improving maternal and infant health through advanced biomarkers, multi-omics, and translational research. This is a  1-year position  with a competitive salary, comprehensive health benefits, conference travel support, and the possibility of renewal subject to external funding The successful candidate will join a collaborative research environment within the  University of British Columbia ,  BC Women's Hospital + Health Centre , and the  BC Children's Hospital Research Institute , working closely with clinicians, scientists, bioinformaticians, and trainees across obstetrics, neonatology, genomics, immunology, and computational biology. Upon appointment, the Fellow will have access to well-characterized clinical cohorts and existing multi-omics datasets for analysis and publication. They will also contribute to the development of new prospective clinical studies, including the establishment of standardized clinical databases and biospecimen collection pipelines across maternal and neonatal cohorts. The Fellow will be encouraged to develop independent research questions aligned with the program and lead high-impact publications and grant applications. The Fellow may contribute to projects involving: multi-omics analyses (single-cell, cell-free RNA/DNA, methylation, proteomics); biomarker discovery for pregnancy complications and neonatal outcomes; computational biology, machine learning, and predictive modelling; laboratory-based biomarker discovery and validation using maternal and neonatal biospecimens. translational studies integrating laboratory discoveries with clinical implementation. Vancouver is a vibrant coastal city located on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territories of the Coast Salish peoples. The city offers stunning natural beauty with easy access to the Pacific Ocean, Coast Mountains, and world-class outdoor recreation. Known for its diverse population, rich arts and food culture, and progressive values, Vancouver consistently ranks as one of the most livable cities in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES Reporting to  Dr. Mady Salker , the Fellow will: Advance patient-oriented and clinic-based research which includes dissemination of existing data (i.e., publications and presentations) as well as launching new research. Contribute to prospective clinical studies and laboratory workflows, including biospecimen collection, processing, and molecular or biomarker analyses. Collaborate with the multidisciplinary team at BCCHR and WHRI and mentor research trainees (e.g., undergraduate, graduate, postgraduate students). Participate in grant applications, including concept development, analysis of preliminary data, preparing background information, and writing grant sections. Apply for postdoctoral funding from federal, provincial, and other relevant agencies. QUALIFICATIONS Successful applicants will have: A doctoral degree (PhD or equivalent) in biology, biomedicine, molecular biology, women’s health research, or related fields with at least 2 years post-doctoral experience. Experience with women’s, maternal, or infant health research and molecular or cellular laboratory methods; experience with Multiomics and/or bioinformatics would be an asset. Demonstrated expertise with quantitative research methods and analysis. A strong or growing publication record as first author. Excellent organization, ethics/record keeping, time management, and communication skills. Excellent interpersonal skills that support teamwork and professional interactions with patients. Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own. Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion. The expected salary for this position is $55,000 to $60,000, plus benefits. Dr. Salker will cover conference registration, travel, and shared accommodations for at least 1 North American conference, if presenting first author research. The Faculty of Medicine is committed to offering equitable and competitive salaries, commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. APPLICATION PROCEDURE An application package should include :  Cover letter describing (a) relevant experience in women's, maternal, or infant health and laboratory or translational research, (b) personal research interests, (c) reasons for pursuing a postdoctoral fellowship with the Salker research group, and (d) interest in a multi-year fellowship, pending external funding (2-page maximum); A detailed curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications and conference presentations; Names and contact information for 2-3 referees who will be able to provide a letter of reference within 5 business days upon request. Applications should be submitted online at https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs . Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please apply as soon as possible to facilitate timely review of your application. The anticipated start date for this position is November 1, 2026 or on a mutually agreed upon date. We invite applications from qualified candidates who share our commitment to employment equity and inclusive excellence, and we welcome applications from candidates belonging to historically, persistently, or systemically marginalized groups: Indigenous (First Nation, Métis, Inuit) Peoples, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women, 2SLGBTQIA+ people, and trans and non-binary people. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are appreciated, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we are committed to creating an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities).  Confidential accommodations are available on request. Please contact Lygia Siqueira, Assistant HR manager via email at lygia.siqueira@ubc.ca. If you have any questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/workplace-accessibility/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca .    With gratitude, we acknowledge that the University of British Columbia Faculty of Medicine and its distributed programs, which include four university academic campuses, are located on traditional, ancestral and unceded territories of First Nations Peoples and communities around the province. Our Vision: To Transform Health for Everyone. Ranked among the world’s top medical schools with the fifth-largest MD enrollment in North America, the UBC Faculty of Medicine is a leader in both the science and the practice of medicine. Across British Columbia, more than 12,000 faculty and staff are training the next generation of doctors and health care professionals, making remarkable discoveries, and helping to create the pathways to better health for our communities at home and around the world. The Faculty - comprised of approximately 2,200 administrative support, technical/research and management and professional staff, as well approximately 650 full-time academic and over 10,000 clinical faculty members - is composed of 19 academic basic science and/or clinical departments, three schools, and 24 research centres and institutes. Together with its University and Health Authority partners, the Faculty delivers innovative programs and conducts research in the areas of health and life sciences. Faculty, staff and trainees are located at university campuses, clinical academic campuses in hospital settings and other regionally based centres across the province. UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities. As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world. UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Hospital-Site---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR26233": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Department Maxwell Laboratory | Department of Paediatrics | Faculty of Medicine (Christopher Maxwell) Posting End Date November 4, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. This position is subject to the satisfactory completion of required background checks Job End Date October 11, 2027     This position is subject to the satisfactory completion of required background checks. Postdoctoral Fellow Position – Cell Division and Mammary/Epithelial/Immune Cancer Biology Maxwell Lab | BC Children’s Hospital Research Institute | University of British Columbia Position Summar y The Maxwell Laboratory invites applications for a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow with strong expertise in mammary gland biology, epithelial tissue organization and immune interactions, or mechanisms of cell division and mitosis, with a particular interest in cancer susceptibility and prevention. The successful candidate will have an excellent publication track record and will join a dynamic research team focused on understanding how errors in cell division and tissue organization contribute to hereditary and childhood cancers, with emphasis on BRCA1-associated pathways, spindle orientation, and epithelial cell fate decisions in mammary and related tissues. About the Lab The Maxwell Lab, led by Dr. Chris Maxwell at BC Children’s Hospital Research Institute and UBC, investigates how cell division is regulated at the molecular and tissue level, and how its disruption alters epithelial – immune interactions that contribute to hereditary cancers and pediatric malignancies (https://bcchr.ca/maxwell-lab). Key areas include: • Mechanisms of cell division fidelity, spindle orientation, and cell polarity • Role of BRCA1 and associated pathways in mammary epithelial biology • Development of in vivo and ex vivo models of pediatric and hereditary cancers • Translation toward personalized medicine approaches in pediatric and hereditary cancers The lab has extensive expertise in: • Human primary epithelial cell systems • Mouse and genetically engineered models • High-content and live-cell imaging • Tumor evolution and therapeutic response modeling Research Environment The fellow will be embedded in a highly collaborative environment within BC Children’s Hospital Research Institute, UBC Department of Pediatrics, and the Michael Cuccione Childhood Cancer Research Program. The lab also actively collaborates with national and international partners in the fields of: Pediatric and hereditary cancer biology and modeling; Oriented cell division mechanisms; and Precision oncology initiatives. Project Focus (Representative Areas) and Responsibilities Applicants may contribute to one or more of the following: 1. Epithelial cell fate and division orientation • Mechanisms controlling symmetric vs asymmetric division • Role of spindle orientation in differentiation and tumor initiation 2. Epithelial – immune cell interactions in hereditary cancer syndromes • Functional consequences of BRCA1 pathway perturbation • Novel strategies for cancer prevention 3. Cell division regulation in epithelial tissue architecture 4. Translational cancer modeling • Patient-derived mammary epithelial systems • In vivo modeling of cancer predisposition states The fellow will be responsible for conducting basic/translational research in one or more of the above research focus areas, design and execute research plans and perform data analyses, contribute to writing manuscripts and grants, present research findings in internal and external meetings, and mentor junior trainees in the laboratory. Required qualifications 1. PhD in Cell Biology, Cancer Biology, Developmental Biology, Biochemistry, or related field. The ideal candidate should have obtained their PhD within the last 2 years and possess the ability to work independently and demonstrate creativity. 2. Strong expertise in at least one of the following: Epithelial biology or organoid systems; Epithelial-Immune cell interactions; Cell division / mitosis / spindle dynamics 3. Proven record of first-author publications in high-impact journals 4. Experience with at least one of the following: Live-cell imaging or advanced microscopy; Mouse models or genetically engineered systems; Primary cell culture or organoid systems; Molecular perturbation (CRISPR, RNAi, etc.) Preferred qualifications • Background in epithelia – immune interactions • Experience in epithelial polarity or tissue architecture • Demonstrated ability to lead projects independently • Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own. • Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion. What We Offe r • Opportunity to work on fundamentally important questions in cancer biology and prevention • Integration in multiple national and international interdisciplinary research teams, including early phase clinical studies • Access to human tissue models and state-of-the-art imaging platforms • Strong mentorship toward academic independence or research scientist careers • The expected salary for this position is $65,000 per annum. The Faculty of Medicine is committed to offering equitable and competitive salaries, commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. Application procedure To apply, please submit the following as one PDF file via https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs (JR26233): • Curriculum Vitae (including publication list) • Cover letter describing research interests, career goals, and a brief summary of prior research accomplishments (1–2 pages) • Contact information for three referees Should you have any queries around this position, please contact Brenda Tse at brenda.tse@ubc.ca Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis and will continue until the position is filled. The start date for this position will be a date to be mutually agreed. We invite applications from qualified candidates who share our commitment to employment equity and inclusive excellence, and we welcome applications from candidates belonging to historically, persistently, or systemically marginalized groups: Indigenous (First Nation, Métis, Inuit) Peoples, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women, 2SLGBTQIA+ people, and trans and non-binary people. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are appreciated, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we are committed to creating an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities). Confidential accommodations are available on request. Please contact Brenda Tse via email at Brenda.tse@ubc.ca. If you have any questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/workplace-accessibility/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca. With gratitude, we acknowledge that the University of British Columbia Faculty of Medicine and its distributed programs, which include four university academic campuses, are located on traditional, ancestral and unceded territories of First Nations Peoples and communities around the province. Our Vision: To Transform Health for Everyone. Ranked among the world’s top medical schools with the fifth-largest MD enrollment in North America, the UBC Faculty of Medicine is a leader in both the science and the practice of medicine. Across British Columbia, more than 12,000 faculty and staff are training the next generation of doctors and health care professionals, making remarkable discoveries, and helping to create the pathways to better health for our communities at home and around the world. The Faculty - comprised of approximately 2,200 administrative support, technical/research and management and professional staff, as well approximately 650 full-time academic and over 10,000 clinical faculty members - is composed of 19 academic basic science and/or clinical departments, three schools, and 24 research centres and institutes. Together with its University and Health Authority partners, the Faculty delivers innovative programs and conducts research in the areas of health and life sciences. Faculty, staff and trainees are located at university campuses, clinical academic campuses in hospital settings and other regionally based centres across the province. UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities. As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world. UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Research-Associate_JR26359": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nJob Title Research Associate Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Cullis Laboratory | Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology | Faculty of Medicine (Pieter Cullis (Retired - Active)) Job End Date November 30, 2027 Posting End Date November 18, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected salary for this position is $ 76,128 . The Department of Biochemistry & Molecular Biology at the University of British Columbia will be hiring a Research Associate at 100% FTE to join the laboratory of Dr. Pieter Cullis.        For more than fifty years, the Department of Biochemistry & Molecular Biology has played an active and important role at UBC and in the greater scientific community. The Department is home to approximately 90 research laboratories from fifteen Departments organized into nine research clusters, fostering a strong foundation for innovation and collaborations. All members of the Department maintain active, well-funded research programs that encompass many areas of modern biochemistry, and molecular and structural biology. The Department hosts an active Graduate program with more than 90 students and offers over fifteen undergraduate courses and laboratory courses for honours, major and minor Undergraduate programs.     Responsibilities will include but are not limited to the following:    Designing and synthesizing RNA, producing nanoparticle formulations, conducting in vitro and in vivo analysis of formulations, and working with collaborators to analyze in vivo samples using flow cytometry, cytokine assays and single cell RNA sequencing. Additional responsibilities include data analysis, preparation of scientific manuscripts, and presentation of research findings at group meetings and scientific conferences.     The successful candidate should have the following:   PhD and Post-doctoral work related to RNA therapeutics, drug delivery, nanomedicine, and a solid scientific and technical background.   Excellent communication skills.   Ability to work in a fast-paced and demanding team environment.   Has the drive and enthusiasm to lead and work as a member of a team.     Candidates interested must apply via the UBC Careers website .     UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities   As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world.       UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.     Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence.  An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged.  We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Black-Faculty-Position---Faculty-of-Science-at-the-University-of-British-Columbia--Vancouver---Assistant-Professor_JR26301-1": {
      "title": "Black Faculty Position – Faculty of Science at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver – Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nJob Title Black Faculty Position – Faculty of Science at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver – Assistant Professor Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Faculty and Staff | Department of Statistics | Faculty of Science (Paul Gustafson) Job End Date Ongoing Posting End Date Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected salary range for this position is CAD $120,000 to $150,000. UBC’s campuses are located on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territories of the Syilx (Okanagan) Peoples and of the Coast Salish Peoples, including the territories of the xwməθkwəy̓əm (Musqueam), Skwxwú7mesh (Squamish), and Stó:lō and Səl̓ílwətaʔ/Selilwitulh (Tsleil- Waututh) Nations.    The Opportunity    The Faculty of Science at the University of British Columbia (UBC) in Vancouver seeks to recruit an accomplished scholar who self-identifies as Black for an open faculty tenure-stream position at the level of Assistant Professor with an expected start date of 1 July 2027. The expected salary range for this position is CAD $120,000 to $150,000. The starting salary will be determined based on the successful candidate's qualifications, experience, rank, and discipline.     This open search hire forms part of the Black Faculty Cluster hiring and the candidate will be joining four recently hired scholars to the cluster, bringing the total number of UBC black faculty members in the Faculty of Science to eleven.  We seek a candidate for this cluster hire initiative in Quantitative Science , broadly defined across the Faculty of Science. This opportunity forms part of the University wide Black Faculty Cohort Hiring Initiative .    Given existing strengths, UBC is well-positioned to become a leader in Quantitative Science, which explicitly includes a diversity of thoughts and perspectives. This open search across the Faculty of Science is the first of its kind, where the onus is on the candidate to demonstrate how their research vision, past, current and future, and their academic profile align with the broader definition of Quantitative Science. With this in mind, the Faculty of Science seeks to recruit a candidate in any of the research areas within the following departments/units who have committed to hosting the candidate (but are not limited to): Computer Science , Institute for the Oceans and Fisheries (IOF) , Institute for Resources Environment and Sustainability (IRES) , Michael Smith Laboratory (MSL) , Physics and Astronomy , Pure Mathematics , Statistics , and  Zoology . Except for Pure Mathematics ( where candidates are not encouraged to demonstrate interdisciplinarity ), candidates whose research spans across one or more departments and/or units are strongly encouraged. For example, candidates whose research lies at the interfacial boundary between any two of the units listed above are highly encouraged to apply. Priority and preference will be given to recruiting an excellent scholar in Quantitative Science over filling a particular research niche.     The Black Faculty Cluster will provide unique structures that support the continued success of the Quantitative Science scholar once they arrive at UBC, including: creating a peer-mentorship group program to facilitate connections among new hires as well as between incoming and current Black scholars; providing research support in the form of postdoctoral fellows co-supervised by 2 or more members of the Black Faculty Cluster; guaranteeing a graduate stipend for each incoming research faculty to support the growth of their lab or research program; and making available resources on transitioning to and navigating life at UBC and in Vancouver as Black people.     The position requires a minimum of a PhD or equivalent in computer science, ecology, fisheries, mathematics, oceanography, physics, statistics, zoology, or related disciplines.  The candidate should be a creative, innovative scientist, with demonstrated research accomplishments and evidence of commitment to effective teaching.  They will be expected to maintain a dynamic externally funded research program, and to take active roles in teaching and the supervision of trainees (undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral researchers). They will have a strong commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion , to create a welcoming community for all, particularly those who are historically, persistently or systemically marginalized.    The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing.   Candidates will be appointed in a UBC Faculty of Science department or research institute appropriate to their disciplinary expertise. Joint appointments across departments/institutes will also be possible.  For more information on the Faculty of Science departments and institutes, see https://science.ubc.ca/departments and links therein.    This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits .    Application Process    Pursuant to Section 42 of the BC Human Rights Code, this search will be restricted to qualified Black scholars. We welcome applications from Black scholars who may also identify as Indigenous (First Nation, Métis, Inuit) Peoples, multi-racial persons, persons with disabilities, women, and/or members of 2SLGBTQIA+ communities. Candidates are required to self-identify through the Applicant Diversity Survey (see link below), which takes approximately two minutes to complete. All questions are voluntary, with an option to decline to answer. Applicants who wish to be considered for this initiative must self-identify as ‘Black’ to be considered eligible. All information collected by the Equity & Inclusion Office (EIO) will remain confidential and any reported data will be in aggregate form shared with the Search Committee co-chairs to track intersectional diversity and support an equitable and meritorious search process.     Applications must be submitted through Academic Jobs Online (AJO) Portal . Interested applicants must submit all of the following documents:   Cover letter: A research vision, past, current and future and alignment with Quantitative Science as well as indicating which Department(s) they interested in joining (1 page).  Current CV, including a list of publications.  Research statement (up to 3 pages single-spaced) describing your work to date and future plans including any interdisciplinary research related to Quantitative Science.  A teaching statement (1 page).  UBC aspires to promote inclusive excellence by supporting and recognizing efforts to advance equity, diversity and inclusion as well as decolonization (EDID) through the academic and research ecosystem. Provide a brief (1 page) statement describing current and future commitments or interests related to EDID. Your comments may relate to lived/living experience, professional work or practice, academic and research activities, and/or community-engagement.  Applicants to this position are asked to complete this equity diversity survey ( https://ubc.ca1.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_71emR2cibNmk8EC ) as part of the application, and candidates must self-identify as a Black person to be considered. Because the search is limited to those self-identifying as Black, candidates must also provide their name on the survey to be considered.  Arrange for three letters of reference by uploading names and contact emails of three references to the AJO portal. The referees will receive automatic emails with instructions to submit their letters. References must submit their letters (on letterhead) directly to the AJO platform by the same deadline of the application.     The closing date for applications is December 1, 2026 . All materials, including reference letters, must be uploaded to the AJO portal by this date.  Information sessions for those who want to learn more about the positions will be held online on the following dates:  Friday, October 16, 10:00 am-11:00 am Pacific time. Register Here   Friday, October 30, 10:00 am-11:00 am Pacific time. Register Here     Any inquiries may be addressed to Anotida Madzvamuse , Anotida.Madzvamuse@ubc.ca .     All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority for the position. To comply with the Government of Canada’s reporting requirements, the University gathers information about applicants’ status as either a permanent resident of Canada or Canadian citizen. Applicants need not identify country of origin or current citizenship; however, all applications must include one of the following statements:           Yes, I am a citizen or permanent resident of Canada           No, I am not a citizen or permanent resident of Canada  Career Interruptions  UBC acknowledges that certain circumstances may cause career interruptions that legitimately affect an applicant’s record of research achievement. We encourage applicants to note in their applications whether they would like consideration given to the impact of any such circumstances due to health, family, or other legitimate reasons to allow for a fair assessment of their research productivity.    Commitment to Accessibility and Accommodations  UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence.  An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged.  Inclusion is built by individual and institutional responsibility through continuous engagement with diversity to inspire people, ideas, and actions for a better world. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Métis, Inuk, or Indigenous person. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible work environment for all members of its workforce. Within this hiring process we will make efforts to create an accessible process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities). Confidential accommodations are available on request by contacting Svetlana Minchenko, minchenko@science.ubc.ca .      If you have any needs or questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the job application, recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Center For Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/working-injury-illness-or-disability/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca ."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Fellow-in-Complex-In-Vitro-Modeling-and-Drug-Formulation-Testing_JR26330": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Complex In Vitro Modeling and Drug Formulation Testing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Fellow in Complex In Vitro Modeling and Drug Formulation Testing Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Research | Kalra Lab | Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Jessica Kalra) Job End Date December 31, 2027 Posting End Date November 15, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected salary amount for this position is $60,000 CAD annually. Institution: University of British Columbia (UBC)/Langara College Location: Vancouver, BC, Canada Department: Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Applied Research Center Langara College Term: Full-time, 1-year initial appointment (renewable based on funding and performance) The Opportunity We are seeking a highly motivated and innovative Postdoctoral Fellow to join our interdisciplinary research team. Our lab focuses on bridging the gap between benchtop drug discovery and clinical translation through the development of advanced physiological models. In this role, you will lead projects focused on designing, validating, and using complex in vitro models (such as organoids, spheroids) to evaluate the efficacy, toxicity, and cellular uptake of novel therapeutic formulations (e.g., lipid nanoparticles, polymeric carriers). You will work in a highly collaborative environment, interacting directly with pharmaceutical scientists, and industry collaborators. Key Responsibilities ●      Model Development: Design, optimize, and characterize 3D cell culture systems, multi-cellular spheroids, or organ-on-a-chip platforms that accurately mimic human tissue microenvironments. ●      Formulation Testing: Evaluate the biodistribution, and cellular mechanisms of novel drug delivery systems using these complex in vitro models. ●      Assay Design: Develop high-throughput screening assays, utilizing flow cytometry, confocal microscopy, and molecular biology techniques (qPCR, Western blotting, ELISA). ●      Data Leadership: Analyze complex datasets, prepare rigorous scientific reports, and present findings at national and international conferences. ●      Mentorship: Provide technical guidance and mentorship to graduate (MSc/PhD) and undergraduate researchers in the lab. ●      Writing and Presenting: Lead the preparation of high-impact peer-reviewed publications and contribute to other knowledge translation and knowledge mobilization activities. Qualifications Required ●      Ph.D. (completed within the last 2 years or expected shortly) in Bioengineering, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Cell/Molecular Biology, Biotechnology, or a closely related field. ●      Demonstrated expertise in mammalian cell culture, specifically with complex in vitro systems (3D cultures, primary cells, co-cultures, or stem cell-derived organoids). ●      Hands-on experience characterizing and testing drug formulations or nanomedicines in vitro . ●      Record of scientific productivity, evidenced by first-author publications in reputable, peer-reviewed journals. ●      Excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Preferred ●      Familiarity with advanced imaging modalities (e.g., live-cell confocal imaging, multi-photon microscopy). ●      Familiarity with cannabinoids and cannabinoid formulations ●      Background in the synthesis or physical characterization of drug delivery vectors (e.g., DLS, zeta potential). What We Offer ●      Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience, aligning with Canadian Institutes of Health Research (CIHR) and UBC Postdoctoral guidelines. The expected salary amount for this position is $60,000 CAD annually. ●      Professional Development: Access to UBC’s Postdoctoral Fellows Office (PDFO) resources, including grant writing workshops, career coaching, and leadership training. ●      Environment: Access to world-class core facilities (imaging, flow cytometry, sequencing, and nanofabrication) nestled in the vibrant, highly livable city of Vancouver. ●      Benefits: This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits How to Apply Interested candidates should submit a single PDF document containing: 1.    A cover letter (max 2 pages) detailing your research interests, how your expertise aligns with this position, and your career goals. 2.    A current Curriculum Vitae (CV) including a complete list of publications. 3.    Contact information for three professional references (including your Ph.D. supervisor). Review of applications will begin on November 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person.   The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing.   All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow--Lipid-Based-Delivery-Systems-and-Advanced-Skin-Permeation-Models_JR26334-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow: Lipid-Based Delivery Systems and Advanced Skin Permeation Models",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow: Lipid-Based Delivery Systems and Advanced Skin Permeation Models Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Research | Kalra Lab | Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Jessica Kalra) Job End Date December 31, 2027 Posting End Date November 15, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected salary amount for this position is $60,000 CAD annually. Institution: University of British Columbia (UBC)/Langara College Location: Vancouver, BC, Canada Department: Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Applied Research Center Langara College Term: Full-time, 1-year initial appointment (renewable based on funding and performance) About the Role The University of British Columbia is seeking a highly motivated and innovative Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join our interdisciplinary research team. This fully-funded position focuses on the design, formulation, and evaluation of advanced lipid-based delivery systems targeting dermal and transdermal applications. The successful candidate will lead projects centered on developing novel lipidic nanocarriers (such as liposomes and LNPs) and testing their transport properties across biological barriers. You will use synthetic membranes, biomimetic permeation systems, and bioengineered 3D skin models to evaluate formulation efficacy, kinetics, and biocompatibility. Key Responsibilities ●      Formulation & Characterization: Design, optimize, and scale the production of lipid-based drug delivery systems using techniques such as microfluidics, thin-film hydration, and high-pressure homogenization. Characterize formulations using DLS, Zeta potential, cryo-TEM, and HPLC. ●      Permeation Studies: Design and execute transport/permeation assays using Franz diffusion cells with synthetic membranes and lipid-based biomimetic barriers (e.g., PAMPA). ●      3D Skin Modeling: Culture, maintain, and use 3D reconstructed human epidermis (RhE) or full-thickness human skin equivalent models to evaluate the dermatokinetics, efficacy, and localized toxicity of the lipid formulations. ●      Data Translation: Correlate data between synthetic models and 3D biological models to establish robust predictive frameworks for transdermal delivery. ●      Leadership: Prepare high-impact manuscripts for peer-reviewed journals, present findings at international conferences, and mentor graduate and undergraduate students in the laboratory. Qualifications ●      A Ph.D. (completed within the last 2 years) in Pharmaceutical Sciences, Biomedical Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Chemistry, or a closely related discipline. ●      Demonstrated expertise in the physical chemistry and manufacturing of lipid-based delivery vehicles. ●      Hands-on experience with in vitro permeation testing and release kinetics modeling. ●      Required specialized knowledge: Proven experience working with mammalian cell culture, specifically the development, handling, and application of 3D skin equivalents/models for pharmacological testing. ●      Strong analytical skills, including proficiency with chromatography (HPLC/UPLC) and spectroscopic techniques. ●      A track record of first-author publications in relevant peer-reviewed journals. ●      Strong written and oral communication skills in English, with the ability to work collaboratively in a diverse, multidisciplinary team environment. What We Offer ●      Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience, aligning with Canadian Institutes of Health Research (CIHR) and UBC Postdoctoral guidelines. ●      Professional Development: Access to UBC’s Postdoctoral Fellows Office (PDFO) resources, including grant writing workshops, career coaching, and leadership training. ●      Environment: Access to world-class core facilities (imaging, flow cytometry, sequencing, and nanofabrication) nestled in the vibrant, highly livable city of Vancouver. ●      Benefits: This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits How to Apply Interested candidates should submit a single PDF document containing: 1.    A cover letter (max 2 pages) detailing your research interests, how your expertise aligns with this position, and your career goals. 2.    A current Curriculum Vitae (CV) including a complete list of publications. 3.    Contact information for three professional references (including your Ph.D. supervisor). Review of applications will begin on November 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person.   The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing.   All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Research-Associate-in-Mammalian-Regeneration_JR26297": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Mammalian Regeneration",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Research Associate in Mammalian Regeneration Department Research | Miller | Michael Smith Laboratories | Faculty of Science (Freda Miller) Posting End Date November 15, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date December 31, 2027     The expected pay range for this position is $76,128 per year.   Research Associate in Mammalian Regeneration at UBC   The Michael Smith Laboratories at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver, Canada, invites applications for a Research Associate position in Mammalian Regeneration to work under the supervision of Dr. Freda Miller. The successful candidate will work on a project defining molecular mechanisms that regulate mammalian digit tip regeneration.   Project : The digit tip is one of the few privileged mammalian tissues that undergo multi-tissue regeneration following injury. For this project, the Research Associate will work with other researchers in the Miller laboratory to define the cellular and molecular mechanisms that confer this unique privileged status upon the digit tip. The Research Associate will use single cell spatial transcriptomics, genetic mouse models, and cutting-edge imaging technologies to pursue this work.   Candidate: The ideal candidate will hold a PhD in biology, molecular biology or other related biomedical sciences field. The candidate must have experience in the area of regeneration biology, and postdoctoral experience with single cell spatial transcriptomics and genetic mouse models as applied to mammalian tissue regeneration. The candidate must be able to work well on a team.   Opportunity: The University of British Columbia is located in Vancouver, which is world-renowned for its scenery and quality of living. The successful applicant will join a vibrant, collaborative environment at UBC’s Michael Smith Laboratories and will benefit from opportunities to engage with world-class researchers in in the field of stem cells and regenerative biology.   The expected pay range for this position is $76,128 per annum. The initial appointment will be for one year commencing January 1, 2027 with a possibility for extension, subject to satisfactory performance and funding.   Application Process: Interested applicants should submit a CV, list of publications and cover letter that details their experience to the UBC Careers portal. Application materials should be submitted no later than the posting end date.   Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Canada-Impact--Emerging-Leader-in-Advanced-Manufacturing_JR26241": {
      "title": "Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader in Advanced Manufacturing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Title Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader in Advanced Manufacturing Department Leadership and Faculty | Department Mechanical Engineering | Faculty of Applied Science (Steve Feng) Posting End Date November 15, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date June 30, 2031     The expected salary range for this position is $125,000 - $150,000 annually. This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver campus, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position, “Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader in Advanced Manufacturing,” through the Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program. We seek candidates with expertise in one or more areas of autonomous and smart manufacturing or materials processing, including in-process sensing and control; in situ characterization of material transformation; physics-based modelling and digital twins; ultra-precision and nanoscale manufacturing; robotic autonomy; and production system optimization. Application areas of interest include the integration of microelectronic, photonic and quantum devices; biomanufacturing; sustainable energy; and mobility, marine and dual-use production, all sectors in which British Columbia has established industrial strength, but we welcome candidates working in other areas. The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in attracting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders ( https://research.ubc.ca/federal-research-chair-programs/canada-impact-emerging-leaders ) will receive research funding and institutional supports to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas, build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world. Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders are six-year awards of $100,000 per year, renewable once for an additional six years, intended for exceptional emerging scholars who have the potential to become leaders in their fields. Applicants must be an early career researcher and be eligible to hold a full-time, tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor at UBC.  In order for Emerging Leader awards to be successfully renewed for an additional six years following their initial six-year term, candidates will need to demonstrate not only their research excellence, but also the impact of their research outside the academy with effective partnerships, knowledge translation and/ or commercialization that provide social or economic benefits to Canada.  Under the Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program, nominees must be working and residing outside Canada when the associated Eddie Goldenberg Research Chairs of Canada award ( https://www.canada.ca/en/impact-plus-chairs.html ) was formally accepted (August 6th, 2026). Applicants should hold a PhD in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related field, as part of the UBC requirement, and either be registered, or be eligible to register as a Professional Engineer (PEng) with Engineers and Geoscientists British Columbia (see https://www.egbc.ca/Become-a-Member ). They should have demonstrated ability to establish a successful research program, exceptional communication skills and evidence of or demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching. The successful applicant will be expected to lead an independent research program, teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, supervise graduate students (Masters and PhD), and provide service to the University and broader academic and professional community. All of our work is shaped by our values: professionalism and integrity; scholarship and teaching excellence; commitment to students; partnerships and collegiality; initiative, innovation, and willingness to change; community, the environment, and sustainability.  We hold these values as an integral frame of reference to inform our decisions and actions at every level and in every situation.  The successful candidate will share these values. The expected salary range for this position is $125,000 - $150,000 annually. This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits . The successful candidate will be part of the Faculty Association collective bargaining unit. The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing. We invite interested applicants to submit their cover letter, CV, research, teaching and diversity statements, and a list of 3 to 5 referees by November 15, 2026 to ensure full consideration. Acceptance and review of applications will continue until the position is filled.  The successful candidate must start by August 1, 2027.   For questions about the position, please contact Jen Pelletier via email at jen@mech.ubc.ca. Details on Research Statement: Please include both information about your research experience and expertise and about your research plan as a new faculty member, including as appropriate current and potential collaborators (at UBC and elsewhere) and sources of funding. (Up to two pages). Details on Teaching Statement: Your teaching statement should describe your teaching experience at any and all scales from one-on-one tutoring to small group tutorials to teaching large section courses. Also explain your teaching philosophy, including specific examples that have informed the evolution of that teaching philosophy. (Up to two pages). Details on Diversity Statement: UBC's strategic plan identifies inclusive excellence as one of our key priorities. We welcome colleagues with the experiences and competencies that can contribute to our principles of inclusion, equity, and diversity throughout campus life. In your application, please include a statement describing your experience working with a diverse student body and your contributions to creating/advancing a culture of equity and inclusion on campus or within your discipline. (Up to two pages). In alignment with UBC’s commitment to creating an equitable and diverse environment that fosters academic excellence, candidates are encouraged to complete a self-identification survey : https://ubc.ca1.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_exPuSwFzPyvB8Ee . Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence UBC’s Vancouver campus is situated at the tip of Point Grey on the unceded lands of the Musqueam people, surrounded by forest, ocean and mountains.  Vancouver is consistently ranked as one of the most diverse cities in Canada, and one of the most livable cities in the world.  UBC seeks to recruit and retain a workforce that is representative of Vancouver's diversity, to maintain the excellence of the University, and to offer students richly varied disciplines, perspectives and ways of knowing and learning. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are accepted, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we will make efforts to create an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities). If you have any questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Center for Workplace Accessibility website at  https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/workplace-accessibility/centre-workplace-accessibility  or contact the Centre at  workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca . Job applicants requiring confidential accommodation or accessibility, or requests for adaptive measures to participate in the hiring process, please contact Sisi Zhou via email at sisi@mech.ubc.ca. UBC hires based on merit and is committed to employment equity . Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply. UBC recognizes the legitimate impact that leaves (e.g., maternity leave, parental leave, leaves due to illness, leaves due to caring for family members, slowdowns due to chronic illness or disability, or COVID-19 impacts) can have on research achievement and commits to ensuring that leaves are taken into careful consideration during the assessment process. Candidates are encouraged to highlight in their application how career interruptions and personal circumstances have affected their careers. Nominations are subject to review by the Tri-agency Institutional Programs (TIPS) Secretariat, and appointment as a Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader is conditional upon their approval. Consult the Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader recruitment  website https://www.canada.ca/en/impact-plus-chairs/program-details/competition/2026/requirements-for-recruitment.html for further details"
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Research-Associate-in-Next-Generation-RNA-Therapeutics-at-UBC_JR26295-1": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Next-Generation RNA Therapeutics at UBC",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Title Research Associate in Next-Generation RNA Therapeutics at UBC Department Research | Blakney | Michael Smith Laboratories | Faculty of Science (Anna Blakney (On Leave)) Posting End Date November 14, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date May 17, 2029     The expected pay for this position is $80,764 per year. The Michael Smith Laboratories at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver, Canada, invites applications for a Research Associate position in Next-Generation RNA Therapeutics to join a dynamic and multidisciplinary team under the co-supervision of Dr. Anna Blakney and Dr. Jay Kizhakkedathu. The successful candidate will work on a project to develop new polymeric strategies for delivery of RNA. The Blakney Lab at UBC ( blakneylab.msl.ubc.ca ) develops next-generation RNA vaccines and therapies based on self-amplifying RNA and using interdisciplinary approaches including bioengineering, immunology, molecular biology and bioinformatics. The Kizhakkedathu lab ( https://cbr.ubc.ca/our-people/investigators/jayachandran-kizhakkedathu/ ) develops novel polymers and biomaterials as therapeutics and devices. Project : RNA medicines have gained considerable momentum in the past few years with the approval of Onpattro and the COVID-19 messenger RNA vaccines. While lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) are the state-of-the-art delivery system for RNA, we recently discovered that polymeric nanoparticles are less inflammatory and induce higher protein expression than LNPs. For this project, we are keen to explore the polymer design space for RNA delivery for a variety of applications. The PDF will work with the Blakney and Kizhakkedathu labs to design and synthesize novel polymers, and test them for RNA delivery and immunogenicity in vivo in preclinical models. We anticipate that polymers are a clinically translatable delivery system for RNA therapeutics. Candidate: We are hiring polymer chemists with a background in polymer synthesis, organic chemistry and or lipid synthesis. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in chemistry, biomedical engineering, chemical engineering, or similar fields with excellent publication record. The candidate must be able to work well on a team, even in situations where an opportunity is more exciting and time-sensitive than normal. We are seeking a creative individual with great interpersonal skills and excellent communication, both written and verbal. Opportunity: This is a unique opportunity for several reasons. First, you will be a member of a fun, diverse and interdisciplinary group. Second, you will learn about RNA gene therapies and different aspects of its delivery. Third, you will be actively involved in projects that are intended for clinical translation. Finally, the University of British Columbia is located in Vancouver, which is world-renowned for its scenery and quality of living. UBC is a center of excellence for growing your career in nanomedicine and gene delivery technologies, and you will have opportunities to collaborate with world-class leaders in the field through the Nanomedicine Innovation Network. Application Process: Interested applicants should submit a CV and cover letter that details their experience and contact details for 3 references. Application materials should be submitted to the UBC Careers portal no later than the posting end date. The appointment will be for two years commencing May 18, 2027 with a possibility for extension, subject to satisfactory performance and funding. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Assistant-Professor-or-Associate-Professor-in-Computer-Engineering_JR26266": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Title Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Computer Engineering Department Department Head | Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering | Faculty of Applied Science (Steven Wilton) Posting End Date December 6, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date June 30, 2031     Please note that applications must be submitted online at https://apply.ece.ubc.ca in order to be considered. Applications submitted solely through Workday will not be reviewed. The expected salary range is $155,000 to $165,000 per year for an assistant professor and $180,000 to $200,000 per year for an associate professor. The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at the University of British Columbia's Vancouver campus invites applications for a full-time position at the rank of assistant professor (tenure-track) or associate professor (tenure-track or tenured) in the area of computer engineering, with a research focus on computing systems.   We interpret computing systems broadly and welcome applications from candidates working in areas including, but not limited to: computer architecture; digital and integrated systems; embedded and cyber-physical systems; hardware/software co-design; operating and runtime systems; compilers; distributed, cloud, and edge systems; networking; data-intensive systems and storage; and hardware and systems security. We particularly welcome candidates whose work crosses traditional boundaries within computer engineering or connects computing systems with emerging application domains.   RESPONSIBILITIES   The successful candidate will develop an independent, internationally recognized research program that attracts external research support and complements and extends the department's existing strengths; teach undergraduate and graduate courses in computer engineering, including foundational courses in areas such as computer organization, digital systems, embedded computing, software systems, and operating systems, as well as specialized courses aligned with their research; supervise graduate and undergraduate students; and contribute service to the department, university, and the profession.   REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS   Applicants must meet the following qualifications:   A doctoral degree in computer engineering, computer science, or a closely related field, completed by the anticipated start date. A record of research excellence, or clear potential to establish an internationally recognized research program, as demonstrated by publications in leading computing systems venues. Evidence of strong teaching ability, or demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching, at both undergraduate and graduate levels, as well as demonstrated ability or potential to provide effective supervision of graduate students. Evidence of service to the academic and professional community, or demonstrated potential to engage in academic and professional service. Hold, or be eligible to obtain registration as a Professional Engineer (P.Eng.) or Limited Licence with Engineers and Geoscientists British Columbia (EGBC) within five years of appointment. Licensure is not required at the time of application. The department has extensive experience supporting new faculty, including those trained outside Canada or holding degrees in computer science, through this process and will provide guidance and support.   In assessing applications, UBC recognizes the legitimate impact that leaves (e.g., parental leave, family responsibilities, leave due to illness) can have on an applicant’s record of research achievement. These impacts will be taken into careful consideration during the assessment process. UBC's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing .   APPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS   All applications must be submitted online at https://apply.ece.ubc.ca . Applicants should submit the following (suggested page lengths are provided as guidance only):   a cover letter (1-2 pages) describing their suitability for the position; a current curriculum vitae; a research statement (3-4 pages) describing past contributions, future research directions, and opportunities for collaboration and research support at UBC and beyond; a teaching statement (2 pages) describing their teaching philosophy (including specific examples that have informed their teaching philosophy), courses taught, teaching interests, and other educational activities; a diversity statement (1-2 pages) describing your experience with and approach to fostering an inclusive environment in research, teaching, mentoring, and/or service, and how you would contribute to these efforts at UBC; and the names and contact information (including email addresses) of four referees who may be contacted to submit references. In alignment with UBC's commitment to fostering an equitable and inclusive environment that supports academic excellence, applicants are invited to complete the following self-identification survey:   https://ubc.ca1.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_9t3bwjdnB1HwpAW   Participation is voluntary. Responses will be used only for equity monitoring purposes and will not be considered in the evaluation of applications.   The deadline for applications is December 7, 2026, at 11:59 p.m. Pacific Time.   The anticipated start date for this position is July 1, 2027, or a date to be mutually agreed upon. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The expected salary range is $155,000 to $165,000 per year for an assistant professor and $180,000 to $200,000 per year for an associate professor. QUIRED This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, please see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits .   For more information about the position or application process, please contact recruiting@ece.ubc.ca.   EQUITY AND INCLUSION   Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the BC Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Métis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority.   UBC is committed to creating and maintaining an equitable and inclusive work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment is one in which differences are valued, recognized, and integrated into all aspects of university life, including planning and decision-making. In this hiring process, we are committed to providing a fair and equitable experience for all candidates, including persons with disabilities. Accommodations are available upon request throughout every stage of the selection process. To confidentially request accommodations, please contact recruiting@ece.ubc.ca.   ABOUT THE DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRICAL AND COMPUTER ENGINEERING   The University of British Columbia is among the top three universities in Canada and the top 50 worldwide in major international rankings, and placed 29th globally in Engineering and Technology in the 2026 QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering is one of UBC's largest academic units, with more than 400 graduate students, 1,100 undergraduate students, and research spanning electrical engineering, computer engineering, and related disciplines.   For computing systems researchers, the pairing of a large ECE department with a strong Department of Computer Science creates unusual breadth: active work at every level of the hardware-software stack, and colleagues in architecture, security, networking, machine learning, and data systems on the same campus. Faculty also draw on research institutes across UBC, including the Institute for Computing, Information and Cognitive Systems (ICICS), the Advanced Materials and Process Engineering Laboratory (AMPEL), and the Stewart Blusson Quantum Matter Institute (QMI).   The department invests in its new faculty. Incoming assistant professors receive a start-up package and dedicated laboratory space, are paired with senior faculty mentors, and have support in recruiting their first graduate students and preparing early grant applications. UBC's Housing, Immigration and Relocation Services supports incoming faculty with relocation, immigration and work permit assistance, dual-career resources for partners exploring positions at UBC and across Greater Vancouver, and priority placement in university childcare.   The department is located on UBC's Point Grey campus in Vancouver, British Columbia, a vibrant and culturally diverse city situated between the Pacific Ocean and the Coast Mountains and consistently ranked among the world's most livable cities. The University of British Columbia and the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering acknowledge the hən̓q̓əmin̓əm̓-speaking xʷməθkʷəy̓əm (Musqueam) people on whose traditional, ancestral, and unceded territory the university resides.   For more information about the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, please visit https://ece.ubc.ca ."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Assistant-Professor_JR26244": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nAcademic Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Title Assistant Professor Department Department Head | Department of Forest Resources Management (Nicholas Charles Coops) Posting End Date November 12, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job End Date Ongoing     The expected salary range for this position is $130,000 to 150,000 CAD. Assistant Professor of the Social Dimensions of Wildfire The Department of Forest Resources Management in the University of British Columbia’s Faculty of Forestry and Environmental Stewardship invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position at the UBC Vancouver campus. The successful applicant is expected to conduct research and teach in fields related to the social dimensions of wildfire. The costs and impacts of wildfire are growing in Canada and are disproportionately affecting rural and Indigenous communities.  Further research is needed to better understand these impacts and to identify effective ways of coexisting with fire, including mitigating the risks that wildfire poses to communities and to other forest values and ecosystem services .     The UBC Faculty of Forestry and Environmental Stewardship is located on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territory of the xʷməθkʷəy̓əm (Musqueam) People. As part of ongoing and University-wide efforts, the Faculty of Forestry and Environmental Stewardship is committed to taking action towards meaningful reconciliation. The UBC Faculty of Forestry and Environmental Stewardship is one of the world’s leading academic institutions in forestry, conducting both national and international research, and providing undergraduate education encompassing Forest Resources Management, Forest Operations, Urban Forestry, Forest Science, Natural Resources Conservation, Bioeconomy Sciences and Technology, and Wood Products Processing. The successful applicant will be an essential part of the Faculty, contributing to the undergraduate and graduate programs.   Required qualifications :   Candidates for this position must have a PhD relevant to the social dimensions of wildfire, or a PhD combined with applied research demonstrating an understanding of the social dimensions relevant to their field of study. The focus of their scholarship should be on the challenges of increasing wildfire and wildfire risk in Canada, opportunities for mitigating those risks, and/or their consequences for society. The successful candidate will have a potential or proven track record of teaching and research that demonstrates:   Expertise with assessing the multi-faceted impacts of wildfire on society and people, including familiarity with social science methods; Experience with or a demonstrated ability to work with Indigenous Peoples, communities, practitioners, decision-makers, NGO’s and other organizations; E xperience with or familiarity with wildfire and with strategies to mitigate its risks in different contexts, whether at the community level, in the broader forested landscape, or from a social and institutional perspective. Demonstrated or strong potential for scientific publishing, knowledge extension and securing research grants.   Desired qualifications:   The ideal candidate will have many of the following qualifications: Experience with social science research methods; Research on wildfire and its social dimensions; Experience working with Indigenous and forest-dependent communities and/or other partners (e.g. industry, NGOs, government); Strong interest or experience in conducting collaborative work, through existing or proposed research, teaching, leadership, service, community engagement, and outreach or other relevant activities; Past experience or the potential to conduct research focused in British Columbia and/or Canada; Evidence or interest in innovative approaches to knowledge mobilization, such as integrated knowledge mobilization, co-creative outputs, and/or contributions to policy. Evidence of, or strong potential for, effective and inclusive teaching and student mentorship at the undergraduate and graduate levels; and A demonstrated record of advancing diversity, equity, and inclusion in a university setting through research, teaching, and service.   How to apply:   Applicants should be submitted via Workday and should include:   Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement (up to 2 pages) describing your current and proposed research program and showing how your work contributes to advancing new ways of addressing the social costs of wildfire and mitigating the risks to Canadian society. Statement (up to 1 page) of teaching philosophy and accomplishments, including specific interests for teaching courses in the UBC Faculty of Forestry and Environmental Stewardship. Statement (up to 1 page) identifying your past/proposed contributions to justice, equity, diversity, and inclusion, along with your ability to work with a diverse student body (giving specific examples where possible). Up to 3 representative publications. Names and contact information for three referees.   The desired starting date for this position is July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Questions about the position may be directed to the Chair of the Search Committee, Dr. Harry Nelson ( Harry.Nelson@ubc.ca ) The expected salary range for this position is $130,000 to 150,000 CAD. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits\\ The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are accepted, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process, we will make efforts to create an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates). Accessibility requests or other accommodations are available on request for all applicants. To confidentially request accommodations, please contact the Faculty of Forestry Associate Dean Equity, Diversity and Inclusion, Dr. Janette Bulkan ( janette.bulkan@ubc.ca )   Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person.   All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Hospital-Site---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR26457": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Wood Laboratory Division of Social Medicine| Department of Medicine | Faculty of Medicine (Evan Wood (On Leave)) Job End Date November 30, 2027 Posting End Date November 26, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. This position is subject to the satisfactory completion of required background checks Please note this is a one (1) year term position. However, there is a possibility of extension. The expected pay range for this position is $60,000 - $75,000 per year. At UBC, we believe that attracting and sustaining a diverse workforce is key to the successful pursuit of excellence in research, innovation, and learning for all faculty, staff and students. Our commitment to employment equity helps achieve inclusion and fairness, brings rich diversity to UBC as a workplace, and creates the necessary conditions for a rewarding career. The full-time Postdoctoral Research Fellow will work under Dr. Evan Wood’s supervision. Dr. Wood is a Principal Investigator with the Canadian Research Initiative in Substance Matters (CRISM) ( https://www.bccsu.ca/about-crism-bc/ ). CRISM is a national research network dedicated to generating, translating, and mobilizing evidence to improve the health and well-being of people who use substances and to strengthen substance use policy, prevention, treatment, harm reduction, and health systems in Canada.   One to two positions are available depending on the calibre of applicants. The successful candidate(s) will join a highly collaborative, interdisciplinary research environment involving researchers, clinicians, health-system partners, community organizations, policymakers, and people with lived and living experience of substance use. The Fellow(s) will contribute to research that addresses priority questions in substance use and substance use-related health, with a particular focus on evaluating clinical and community-based interventions and translating research evidence into clinical practice, guidelines, and guidance documents.   The research program encompasses both quantitative and qualitative research methodologies. Depending on the Fellow's expertise and the needs of the research program, projects may involve epidemiological and population health research, clinical research, health services and health systems research, intervention evaluation, implementation science, qualitative and mixed-methods research, evidence synthesis, and knowledge translation. Experience with systematic review activities and/or guideline activities is an asset.   The Fellow will have opportunities to contribute to the development, implementation, evaluation, and interpretation of research designed to understand what interventions work, for whom, under what circumstances, and how interventions can be effectively implemented in real-world clinical and community settings. Research findings will be used to inform evidence-based clinical practice, policy, clinical guidelines, practice recommendations, and guidance documents for health professionals, health systems, community organizations, and people with lived and living experience of substance use.   The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will support critical research objectives, including: Designing, conducting, and analyzing quantitative, qualitative, and/or mixed-methods research addressing priority questions in substance use and substance use-related health Evaluating the effectiveness, implementation, acceptability, feasibility, equity, and sustainability of clinical and community-based interventions Integrating findings from quantitative and qualitative research to develop a comprehensive understanding of intervention outcomes and implementation Translating research findings into evidence summaries, clinical recommendations, guidelines, guidance documents, policy products, and other knowledge translation outputs Developing and leading peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, reports, and other research products Contributing to evidence reviews, evidence syntheses, and the development and updating of clinical guidance Supporting the development of research proposals, grant applications, and funding strategies Working collaboratively with clinicians, researchers, health-system partners, community organizations, policymakers, and people with lived and living experience of substance use   Position Responsibilities The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will contribute to a portfolio of interdisciplinary research projects and may be responsible for: Developing research questions and study protocols related to substance use, clinical care, harm reduction, treatment, prevention, and health services Designing and conducting quantitative, qualitative, and mixed-methods research Developing and applying appropriate quantitative analytical approaches, including epidemiological, biostatistical, longitudinal, and/or causal methods Designing, conducting, and analyzing qualitative research, including interviews, focus groups, observations, document analysis, and other qualitative approaches, as appropriate Integrating quantitative and qualitative findings to evaluate interventions and inform clinical and health-system decision-making Evaluating clinical and community-based interventions, including their effectiveness, safety, acceptability, feasibility, reach, equity, implementation, and sustainability Working with clinical and health-system partners to identify research questions and ensure that research findings address important real-world clinical and service-delivery needs Conducting literature reviews, systematic reviews, rapid reviews, evidence syntheses, and other forms of evidence assessment as appropriate Assessing the quality, strength, applicability, and limitations of available evidence to support clinical and policy recommendations Contributing to the development, review, and updating of clinical guidelines, practice recommendations, and guidance documents Translating complex research findings into accessible and actionable recommendations for clinicians, health-system leaders, policymakers, community organizations, and people with lived and living experience Leading or co-leading peer-reviewed manuscripts and conference presentations Preparing research reports, evidence summaries, policy briefs, clinical guidance materials, and knowledge translation products Contributing to grant applications and research funding proposals Participating in research team meetings, seminars, conferences, working groups, and other scholarly activities Developing and maintaining collaborative relationships with researchers, clinicians, community organizations, health-system partners, policymakers, and people with lived and living experience Mentoring and supervising research assistants, graduate students, and other trainees, as appropriate Supporting knowledge mobilization activities and dissemination of research findings to academic and non-academic audiences Contributing to the development and maintenance of CRISM's national and regional research collaborations Supporting other research activities required by the program   Research Environment CRISM provides a unique interdisciplinary and collaborative environment for conducting research that bridges research, clinical practice, community-based care, health systems, and policy. The successful candidate will have opportunities to work across disciplines and settings and to engage with investigators and partners working in areas such as substance use treatment, harm reduction, mental health, infectious diseases, pain, overdose prevention, health services, public health, Indigenous health, implementation science, and health policy.   A particular strength of the research environment is the opportunity to move research findings beyond academic publication and into clinical and health-system practice. Fellows will have opportunities to contribute to projects in which evidence is generated through clinical and population research, synthesized and critically appraised, and ultimately translated into recommendations intended to support clinical decision-making and improve care. The position therefore provides an opportunity for a researcher interested not only in generating evidence, but also in understanding how evidence can be evaluated, synthesized, interpreted, and translated into meaningful changes in clinical practice and health-system delivery.   The Fellow will be supervised by Evan Wood and will be based within the British Columbia Centre on Substance Use in Vancouver, BC, on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territory of the xʷməθkʷəy̓əm (Musqueam) peoples.   The successful candidate will work closely with CRISM investigators and collaborators across Canada and will have opportunities to contribute to multidisciplinary research projects, develop an independent research program, publish in peer-reviewed journals, present findings at scientific and community forums, contribute to clinical guidance and guideline development, and develop skills in research leadership, knowledge mobilization, and research funding.   Required Qualifications A PhD in epidemiology, public health, health services research, medicine, nursing, psychology, social sciences, population health, or a related discipline Demonstrated research experience in substance use, mental health, public health, clinical research, health services, health policy, or a related field Demonstrated experience designing, conducting, and analyzing research studies Demonstrated experience with quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods research Experience conducting or contributing to clinical research and/or evaluation of clinical interventions Demonstrated ability to independently develop research questions, conduct analyses, interpret research findings, and communicate results A strong record of peer-reviewed publication appropriate to career stage Strong scientific writing and oral communication skills Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively in an interdisciplinary research environment   Preferred Qualifications Preference may be given to candidates with experience in one or more of the following areas: Substance use research, including research involving people with lived and living experience of substance use Clinical trials, pragmatic trials, observational studies, or other forms of clinical research Evaluation of clinical, health-system, community-based, or public health interventions Epidemiology, biostatistics, longitudinal analysis, causal inference, or other quantitative methods Qualitative research methods, including interviews, focus groups, ethnography, thematic analysis, or qualitative evidence synthesis Mixed-methods research Implementation science and evaluation Health services and health systems research Systematic reviews, rapid reviews, evidence synthesis, or guideline development Development or evaluation of clinical practice guidelines, clinical guidance, or practice recommendations Knowledge translation or knowledge mobilization Experience translating research findings into recommendations for clinicians, health systems, policymakers, or community organizations Experience with large, longitudinal, administrative, clinical, or survey datasets Proficiency with relevant analytical software such as R, Stata, SAS, SPSS, Python, NVivo, or equivalent tools Experience developing grant applications and research funding proposals Community-engaged, patient-oriented, participatory, or Indigenous research approaches Experience working collaboratively with clinicians, health-system partners, policymakers, community organizations, and/or people with lived and living experience   Communications and Collaboration Excellent written and oral communication skills in English and proven ability to work within an interdisciplinary team. Ability to synthesize and critically interpret evidence from diverse research methodologies with an emphasis on critical appraisal and systematic review Ability to translate scientific findings into clear, clinically relevant recommendations and knowledge products Excellent organizational and project-management skills Demonstrated ability to work independently while contributing effectively to multidisciplinary teams Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain productive relationships with clinical, academic, community, and policy partners Commitment to respectful, inclusive, equity-oriented, and meaningful engagement with people with lived and living experience of substance use   Career Development and Training Opportunities This position will provide the successful candidate with opportunities to develop expertise across the full research-to-practice continuum, including: Clinical and health services research Quantitative, qualitative, and mixed-methods research Intervention evaluation and implementation science Evidence synthesis and critical appraisal Clinical guideline and guidance development Research partnership development Scientific writing and publication Grant development and research funding Mentorship and development of an independent research program   The Fellow will be encouraged to develop a research portfolio that supports progression toward an independent research career in substance use, clinical research, health services, public health, or a related field. Applying to the Micheal Smith Foundation, the Canadian Institutes for Health Research or other peer-reviewed post-doctoral funders is encouraged.   Candidates are encouraged to describe their experience with clinical research, intervention evaluation, quantitative and/or qualitative methods, evidence synthesis, clinical guideline development, knowledge translation, and collaborative research. Questions about this position may be directed to Shirley Wong (CRISM BC Senior Project Manager, Shirley.C.Wong@ubc.ca ).   UBC is committed to creating a diverse, inclusive, and equitable environment and encourages applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the BC Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Métis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. Candidates of any nationality are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR26428": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-25\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Upham, Chester | Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering | Faculty of Applied Science (David Chester Upham) Job End Date November 30, 2027 Posting End Date November 29, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected salary for this position is $75,000 CAD per year. Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Sustainable Aviation Fuels   The Upham group in the Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering at the University of British Columbia (Vancouver) is hiring a full time Postdoctoral Fellow to develop a tandem catalytic process that converts syngas to oxymethylene ethers (OMEs) for sustainable aviation fuels. The initial appointment is for 1-year, with the possibility of extension, subject to availability of funds and work performance. This position is based in the research group's laboratories on the UBC Vancouver campus The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027 or a date to be mutally agreed upon.   The research group works on heterogeneous and molten metal catalysis for methane pyrolysis and CO2 conversion, and on carbon valorization. The successful candidate will join a team of graduate students, postdoctoral fellows and undergraduate researchers and will have access to the group's catalyst synthesis, reactors, and characterization facilities.   Project The project is developing a new catalyst to enable a tandem catalytic process in which the steps from syngas to OMEs are coupled rather than run as separate unit operations. OMEs are oxygenated fuel molecules that can be made from syngas derived from biomass, waste or captured CO2 and hydrogen, and are of interest as low-carbon aviation fuel components.   The work is primarily experimental. The postdoctoral fellow will develop and test new catalysts for the individual steps, study how the steps interact when coupled, and demonstrate the integrated process in flow reactors. The results will establish the catalyst performance, product distributions and operating windows needed for process evaluation and scale-up.   Responsibilities Synthesize, characterize and test catalysts for the syngas-to-OME reaction steps Design, build and operate flow reactors Analyze reaction products by gas chromatography and related methods, and quantify conversion, selectivity and yield Plan experiments, interpret data and prepare manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals Help supervise graduate and undergraduate students working on the project Present results at group meetings, to project partners and at conferences Maintain safe laboratory practices and contribute to the group's safety procedures for flammable and toxic gases   Qualifications Required: PhD in chemical engineering, chemistry, materials science or a related field, completed within 5 years of the start date Hands-on experience in heterogeneous catalysis, including catalyst synthesis, characterization and testing in flow reactors Record of peer-reviewed publications in catalysis or reaction engineering Ability to work independently and to guide students in the laboratory   Preferred: Experience with methanol or dimethyl ether synthesis, formaldehyde chemistry, or other oxygenate fuel chemistry Experience with flow reactor systems and process gas handling Experience with reactor modelling or process simulation Experience with in situ or operando catalyst characterization   The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing.   How to apply The application deadline is November 30, 2026. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue through November until the position is filled.   Applicants must include the following items, combined in a single PDF, in their applications: A cover letter describing your research experience and its relevance to this project A curriculum vitae including a list of publications Names and contact information for three references   Direct the application package to both : Dr. Chester Upham ( chester.upham@ubc.ca ) and Hailey Boyer, Group Administrator and Coordinator of The Carbon Hub ( hailey.boyer@ubc.ca ).   Inquiries about the position may be directed to hailey.boyer@ubc.ca   The expected salary for this position is $75,000 CAD per year. This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits   Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. The Department is located on UBC's Point Grey campus in Vancouver, British Columbia, a vibrant and culturally diverse city situated between the Pacific Ocean and the Coast Mountains and consistently ranked among the world's most livable cities. The University of British Columbia and the Department of Chemical and Biological Engineering acknowledge the hən̓q̓əmin̓əm̓-speaking xwməθkwəy̓əm (Musqueam) people on whose traditional, ancestral, and unceded territory the university resides."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Archivist--University-Archives_JR26360": {
      "title": "Archivist, University Archives",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nJob Title Archivist, University Archives Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department UBC Library I Rare Books and Special Collections (Katherine Kalsbeek) Job End Date Ongoing Posting End Date November 22, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected pay (or pay range) for this position is $8034.92 to $13,659.08 per month. UBC LIBRARY As one of the world’s leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world. We honour, celebrate and thank the xʷməθkʷəy̓ əm (Musqueam) and Syilx Okanagan peoples on whose territories the main campuses of the University of British Columbia have the privilege to be situated. The University of British Columbia Library is one of the largest academic libraries in Canada and consistently ranks among the top university research libraries in North America. UBC Library has 14 branches and divisions across two campuses (Vancouver and Kelowna), including an off-site hospital library; a multi-purpose teaching and learning facility, the Irving K. Barber Learning Centre; and the X wi7 x wa Library, a centre for academic and community Indigenous scholarship.  Almost 300 knowledgeable employees – librarians, management and professional staff, support staff and student staff – provide users with the excellent resources and services that they need to further their research, teaching and learning. The UBC Library is committed to being a respectful, healthy environment that encourages leadership, collegiality, diversity, individual growth and opportunity. Explore our aspirational values that we strive to uphold and actively incorporate into all aspects of our organization.  We are committed to eliminating institutional and structural systems of oppression and power (such as colonialism, sexism, classism, heterosexism, ableism, and white supremacy). Learn more about the UBC Library Strategic Framework and about working with us . LIBRARY BRANCH/UNIT The University Archives (UA) serves as the central repository for recorded data in all formats created by, for, and about the University of British Columbia. The University Archives’ mandate is to identify, select, preserve and make available for use the University’s permanently valuable records. POSITION OVERVIEW Reporting to the Head of Rare Books and Special Collections and University Archives, the Archivist, University Archives is responsible for the development and delivery of archival services supporting the University’s permanently valuable records. The Archivist, University Archives leads the identification, acquisition, appraisal, arrangement, description, preservation, and provision of access to archival materials in all formats. The role includes collaborating with the Records Management Archivist to establish transfer procedures and workflows aligned with Board of Governors’ policies and long-term preservation requirements. The Archivist, University Archives provides leadership in the development of archival policies and negotiates acquisitions of private records from faculty, administrators, and affiliated organizations. Responsibilities include overseeing the creation and maintenance of standards-based finding aids, reviewing collections and services to reflect the diversity of the University community, and supervising physical collections management and storage. The position also coordinates reference, instruction, collections development, and outreach activities, and promotes archival holdings through exhibitions and other initiatives. Additional responsibilities include preparing grant applications, developing partnerships, supervising staff and student workers as required, participating in library and campus committees, professional organizations, and the University community. This position works closely with professional and support staff in the Records Management Office, Rare Books and Special Collections, and UBC Okanagan Special Collections and Archives. They may be required to supervise student employees and library staff.  Please visit the UBC careers website to view the full position description and on how to apply. QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED Masters-level degree with a specialization or concentration in archival science. Experience in planning and executing the processing, arrangement, and description of archival materials. Experience working with archival material in all formats, including textual records, audio-visual materials, digitized and born digital materials, publications, maps, and ephemera. Strong written and oral communication skills. Experience in core archival functions and knowledge of archival descriptive standards, particularly the Rules for Archival Description (RAD), and with relevant archival management systems. Demonstrated ability to initiate, plan, and carry out projects, both independently and as a member of a team. Experience building productive relationships and working collegially with diverse populations of employees and patrons in keeping with the UBC Respectful Environment Statement. Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own. Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion. PREFERRED Demonstrated experience working in an academic or public archives environment with a range of materials. Experience with Records Management life-cycle management and records retention schedules. Demonstrated experience with Canadian Copyright legislation and BC’s Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act (FIPPA). Strong interest in and knowledge of British Columbia and Canadian history and current affairs, particularly the histories, cultures, and social development of early British Columbia. Experience, course work, or knowledge of working with Indigenous materials, and awareness of relevant issues around culturally appropriate management of archival and library materials. Familiarity with current trends in instruction and best practices in learning pedagogies. Knowledge/experience with library management systems and library descriptive standards. Evidence of curiosity, ideas, creativity, innovation, pursuit of opportunities, and exploration of new initiatives. Experience or knowledge related to incorporating equity, diversity, and inclusion (EDI) into public services, instruction, collection development, and overall professional practice. TERMS OF APPOINTMENT AND SALARY Appointment : This is a full-time confirmation-track appointment with an anticipated start date of January 2027. The successful candidate will hold an initial three-year appointment, normally followed by an ongoing confirmed appointment. In most cases confirmation-track appointments are reviewed following the second year of appointment, and a recommendation is made at that time to grant or not to grant an ongoing confirmed appointment. The successful candidate will be a member of The University of British Columbia Faculty Association and the terms and conditions of appointment are governed by the Collective Agreement between UBC and the UBC Faculty Association ( http://www.hr.ubc.ca/faculty-relations/collective-agreements/ ). Eligibility: We are only considering applications from librarians with a maximum of 5 years of professional library experience. However, all internal candidates will be considered regardless of years of experience and are encouraged to apply. Benefits: Librarians and their dependents are eligible for an extensive benefits package including extended health care coverage, dental coverage, Employee and Family Assistance Program, life insurance, pension and travel benefits. To support families, UBC provides a top-up for eligible employees on maternity or parental leave. Tuition assistance is also available for qualifying employees and dependent children. In addition, librarians/archivists are eligible for professional development funding to support career growth at the university, as well as study leave. Details are available in the Collective Agreement, and for more information see UBC Human Resources: https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits/eligibility-enrolment/employee-groups . Additional information about relocation to UBC Vancouver can be found: https://hr.ubc.ca/working-ubc/housing-and-relocation-services UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged.  We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.   The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing. APPLICATION PROCESS Applications will include: a detailed and current curriculum vitae; and a letter of application that includes a statement of citizenship/immigration status and indicates the candidate’s education, training and work experience in the areas listed above, as well as how did you hear about this opportunity. One consolidated PDF is preferred."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Co-op-Career-Advisor-and-Industry-Partner_JR26382": {
      "title": "Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nJob Title Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner Job Category M&P - AAPS Job Family Group Staff - Non Union Department Co-op Coordinators I| Experiential Learning and PD |Faculty of Applied Science (Lily Chen) Job End Date November 30, 2027 Posting End Date October 29, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Job Description At UBC, we believe that attracting and sustaining a diverse workforce is key to the successful pursuit of excellence in research, innovation, and learning for all faculty, staff and students. Our commitment to employment equity helps achieve inclusion and fairness, brings rich diversity to UBC as a workplace, and creates the necessary conditions for a rewarding career.  Job Summary The Faculty of Applied Science Co-op Program is responsible for the development, implementation, management and evaluation of Co-operative Education in the Faculty of Applied Science. Co‑operative Education provides students enriched educational experiences for personal and professional growth by working with diverse community partners and sectors to provide transformative workplace learning experiences as part of their Bachelor of Applied Science and Masters of Engineering degree programs in Chemical, Chemical and Biological, Mining, Materials, Manufacturing and Integrated Engineering. Responsibilities of the Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner include the development, implementation, coordination and evaluation of the Applied Science Co-op Program in accordance with national accreditation standards set out by Co-operative Education and Work-Integrated Learning Canada (CEWIL Canada). Tasks include designing and implementing strategies to recruit students, facilitating comprehensive career readiness curriculum including advising and coaching students in preparation for their job search and work experiences, monitoring co-op work placements, addressing workplace issues that students encounter, providing assessment, evaluation and analysis of the placement experience and student success each term, collaborating with other co-op professionals throughout the province and country, and the day-to-day administration of the program. The Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner is also responsible for working directly with existing local, national and international employers. Externally, this role develops and maintains relationships with employers and industry partners to build resources and programs to support undergraduate and graduate career development and learning.  Organizational Status Reports to the Manager, APSC Co-op of the Applied Science Co-op Program. The Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner works closely with faculty, staff, and students to deliver co-op programming for a total of approximately 3,500 Applied Science co-op students during their multi-year journeys as co-op students at UBC. The Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner may supervise student staff positions, including hiring, discipline, evaluation, and termination. Further, they support external employers to provide advice, mediate major workplace issues, and intervene when necessary. Work Performed a.    ADVISING AND COACHING:  •    Provides co-op students with one-on-one and group career advising and coaching before, during and after each Co-op work term.  •    Evaluates and interprets students’ qualifications to suggest relevant job postings and/or employers with the goal of ensuring successful co-op work terms.  •    Identifies opportunities for student learning and promotes student engagement in career building experiences, across the student experience, within and beyond the university.  •    Provides career advising and coaching for students on a variety of career and professional development topics and work search (i.e., career exploration, researching the labour market, making meaning of one’s disciplinary learning, facilitating experiential learning and reflection, gaining work/volunteer/leadership experience, resumes, cover letters, job interviews, networking and connecting to the workplace community).  •    Identifies and provides support when students are in crisis or in need of health support and refers to appropriate level of support or intervention (i.e., Emergency Services, Counseling Services, Early Alert).  •    Provides advice, coaching and resources and advise students facing complex workplaces issues such as discrimination, harassment, bullying and/or termination.   b.    CO-OP WORK TERM OVERSIGHT:  •    Conducts work term site visits and check-ins to monitor and evaluate the technical requirements of the co-op work term placement and the workplace environment with respect to working conditions, safety standards, and academic requirements of the position.  •    Evaluates student performance in the workplace to ensure they are meeting employer needs and addresses any student and/or employer issues. This includes problem solving, mediating, and addressing workplace concerns or issues related to employee discipline, employee rights (including harassment, employment equity, employment standards), intellectual property rights, confidentiality concerns, and other matters raised by students and/or employers. Makes recommendations to Manager, APSC Co-op regarding student’s status in the program as a result of workplace issues.  •    Designs, evaluates and assesses all required work term deliverables such as end of term assignments and employer evaluation of the work term, that must meet national accreditation standards.  •    Assigns grades for co-op courses based on the requirements of the course.    c.    TRAINING:   •    Oversees the design, content, facilitation, and teaching of all co-op pre-employment and career readiness curriculum. This includes, but is not limited to, asynchronous and synchronous curriculum and training events, program orientations, pre-employment training workshops, information sessions, as well as recruiting events that can involve prospective students, current students, and alumni. Evaluates student feedback to determine and implement changes.     d.    EMPLOYER FACING DUTIES  •    Establishes and maintains ongoing relationships with employers and alumni locally, nationally, and internationally that support the creation of jobs.  •    Develops and runs employer events that support student career development.  •    Participates in the development of strategic business plans including conducting market research to identify and target partnerships for ongoing co-op work integrated learning growth. •    Advises and consults employers in developing academically appropriate co-op work opportunities that align with national co-op accreditation requirements, reviews and approves job postings, and writes/edits job descriptions on behalf of employers when requested. Provides advice to employers with recruitment strategies related to diversity and inclusion. Advises employers on funding availability through national and provincial funding programs.  •    Identifies when new or existing co-op employers are not an appropriate partner for hiring co-op students and works with the management team to end the relationship with the employer.    e.    NETWORKING:   •    Attends various events, locally and nationally, such as conferences, trade shows, career fairs, and information sessions. Maintains involvement in relevant industry professional associations and Co-operative Education and Work Integrated Learning associations to expand students’ opportunities to engage with employers, alumni and community.  •    Builds and maintains relationships with alumni, employers and other members of the workplace community to strategically develop resources and programming that initiate connections and provide networking opportunities for students within and beyond UBC.  •    Develops and creates opportunities for students to connect with and learn from alumni, employers, and other members of the workplace community. For example, invite and prepare alumni, employers and industry partners, as subject matter experts, to participate in career development programs.    f.    PROGRAM RECRUITMENT AND ADMISSION  •    Develops and implements a student marketing and recruitment plan to attract new students to apply to the Co-op Program each year.  •    Designs and manages the student admission process, including application, interview and development of admission criteria and scoring systems. Makes final decisions regarding student acceptance into the program.   g.    OPERATIONAL - STRATEGIC PLANNING, ADMINISTRATION, ASSESSMENT, EVALUATION & BUDGETING  •    Contributes to strategic planning, goal setting and prioritization for the program. Oversees and manages Co-op Program strategic projects and continuous improvement initiatives such as revising and updating co-op student pre-employment training curriculum, finding ways to scale the program for more students, and integrate latest technologies such as artificial intelligence.  •    Liaises with campus partners to coordinate and plan joint initiatives as well as to promote the programs.  •    Evaluates the effectiveness of the program, recommends improvements and implements necessary changes.   •    Increases capacity across the program for career learning and career conversations, through multiple points of intersection in the student experience.  •    Participates in the assessment and evaluation of career learning for students. Collects and records statistical information and feedback, analyzes, documents and reports results, integrates suggestions and improvements into programs and services. Prepares reports on student placement statistics that are required for national co-op accreditation.  •    Participates in the development and management of the Co-op Program budget and is responsible for the budget of events and initiatives. Contributes content for budget-related reports that are required by the Dean’s Office.  •    Works closely with UBC’s International Student Advising Office and directs students to follow all legal and immigration policies required.  •    Participates in Faculty, Departmental, UBC-wide and external meetings, committees, and events. •    The Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner assists the Manager, APSC Co-op in areas of strategic planning and operational procedures for the program encouraging (re)development of initiatives and activities that address the changing needs and diversity of the campus. Evaluates trends to recommend, advocates and initiates responsive and proactive change within the program. Exercises individual judgement in organizing work, dealing with students and faculty and other external agencies. Exercises judgement in dealing with employers, faculty members and students.  •    Performs other duties within the guidelines of the role as required.  Consequence of Error/Judgement Incorrect decisions can affect the number and quality of student applicants to the program. Failure to adequately assess employers could result in students getting hired into inappropriate work environments. Failure to assess and prepare students for appropriate technical work placements could result in loss of credibility of the program with industry. Failure to intervene and resolve student work placement performance problems could result in loss of working relationships between industry and the University. Failure to apply policies and procedures established by the Ministry of Post-Secondary Education and Future Skills could result in loss of accreditation and/or financial support.    Ineffective recruitment or career-related advice and support would have a serious negative impact on relationships with UBC students, faculty and departments, employers, and alumni. Dissatisfied prospective and current students and/or alumni may contribute to poor public relations for the University, which in turn would have a negative effect on the University’s ability to raise funds and attract and retain outstanding students. Supervision Received Reports to the Manager, APSC Co-op. Works independently within constraints of policy and administrative framework. Work is subject to review in relation to program goals.   Supervision Given May supervise student staff roles in conjunction with Manager/Associate Directors, and/or student staff. The Co-op Career Advisor and Industry Partner is authorized to make decisions related to student participation in the co-op program and work terms if Terms & Conditions are not adhered to. Assists in training of new professional staff in the Applied Science Co-op Program.  Minimum Qualifications Undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline. Minimum of four years of related experience, or then equivalent combination of education and experience. - Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own - Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion Preferred Qualifications An undergraduate degree, preferably in a Applied Science related discipline; Master’s degree preferred. Knowledge of co-operative education, human resource management, and/or relevant industry experience and knowledge, is preferred. The successful applicant will have excellent oral and written communication skills including presentation skills, and exceptional organizational skills. A passion for work integrated learning and a demonstrated ability to take initiative and work independently as well as within a team is essential. Ability to act with tact, diplomacy, and discretion. Expertise in equity, diversity and inclusion an asset. Knowledge and experience with career advising and coaching, conflict resolution, international work programs, project management, and event planning. Ability to provide quality service to students and employers in a courteous and professional manner. Strong computer skills (Microsoft Office, Teams, OneDrive, Zoom, database management) required and the ability to effectively multi-task and prioritize routine daily work and long-term projects. Valid BC Driver’s License, access to a vehicle, and ability to travel may be required. Must be able to work flexible hours, including occasional evenings and weekends. The Applied Science Co-op team operates with a hybrid work arrangement."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR26379": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Research | Kumar Lab | Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Ujendra Kumar) Job End Date December 31, 2027 Posting End Date November 20, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected salary for this position is $55,000 CAD. Position Details:   Start Date:  January 1, 2027 Term:  The initial appointment is for 1 year with the possibility of renewal subject to availability of funds and work performance Salary:  The expected salary for this position is $55,000 CAD Location:  UBC Vancouver Campus Supervisor:  Dr. Ujendra Kumar Application Deadline:  November 20, 2026   Position Summary:   Application are invited for a full-time postdoctoral position to join our team, focusing on studies related to excitotoxicity in neurodegenerative disease models. The lab’s primary research explores the roles of somatostatin and cannabinoids in these models. Additionally, the lab investigates herbal compounds with potential anti-cancer properties.   The successful candidate will be engaged in research to understand the mechanisms underlying Alzheimer’s disease. Key responsibilities include validating biomarkers, testing small-molecule therapeutics in animal models, and evaluating the neuroprotective and behavioral effects of cannabinoids and somatostatin. Another important aspect of the research involves screening and evaluating herbal compounds using various breast cancer cell lines and in vivo models. Consequences of Error: This position requires professional action on all aspects of research work and is responsible for the production of high quality, reproducible, well-documented research activities. Errors in judgement and failure to report problems have significant consequences and will lead to termination of the position.   Supervision Received:   The Postdoctoral Fellow will work with a high degree of independence and set priorities under directives from Dr. Kumar.   Supervision Given:   This position may involve supervision of undergraduate students, other lab members and collaborators to accomplish research goals.   Minimum Qualifications:   Ph.D. in a relevant field, with expertise in neuroscience and cellular and molecular biology. Proficiency in immunohistochemistry, confocal microscopy, and cell culture, with required experience in in vivo models. Experience in behavioral studies, RNA-Seq analysis, and molecular biology is highly desirable. Strong skills in scientific writing, including manuscript and grant preparation. Experience mentoring students is a plus.   General Responsibilities: Conduct research on assigned projects. Assist in writing grant proposals and research papers. Mentor undergraduate and graduate students, guiding experimental design, analysis etc. Collaborate with research partners and present findings.   Application Requirements:   Cover letter detailing your research experience and qualifications. Academic CV, including a list of publications and Contact information for at least two-three referees (including PhD Supervisor).   Applications including cover letter, curriculum vitae and the contact information of two-three references must be submitted online by November 20, 2026 ( https://hr.ubc.ca/careers-and-job-postings/faculty-careers ). Review of applications will begin on that date. Further information on the Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences is available at www.pharmsci.ubc.ca.   This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits   Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person.   All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Assistant-Professor-in-Hydrometallurgy_JR26350": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Hydrometallurgy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor in Hydrometallurgy Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Administrative Leadership | Department of Materials Engineering | Faculty of Applied Science (Daan Maijer) Job End Date June 30, 2032 Posting End Date January 3, 2027 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected starting salary range for the Assistant Professor position is $120,000 - $130,000 per annum. The Department of Materials Engineering at the University of British Columbia (Vancouver campus) seeks qualified applicants for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in hydrometallurgy, with an emphasis on solvent extraction and ion exchange (in exceptional cases, a tenure-track or tenured, appointment at the rank of Associate Professor may be considered). The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Over its long history, the Department of Materials Engineering at UBC has distinguished itself with excellence in research, teaching and service in Materials Engineering. The Department has a strong record of collaborative work within UBC and industry and promotes interdisciplinary research. This appointment will strengthen solution purification and separation expertise within UBC’s Industrial Research Chair in Hydrometallurgy and support integrated process development for primary and secondary resources. We seek an emerging researcher with expertise in aqueous speciation, chemical equilibria and separation chemistry, and demonstrated expertise in solvent extraction, ion exchange or both. Relevant capabilities include metal–ligand chemistry, extraction and exchange equilibria, reaction kinetics, mass transfer, and the behaviour of complex multicomponent solutions. Research should connect fundamental materials chemistry with process performance, including selectivity, loading and stripping, regeneration, phase behaviour and reagent stability. Potential research directions include selective metal recovery from complex leach solutions; improved extractants, resins and operating strategies; impurity removal; and separations for base and precious metals, critical minerals and recycled materials. The candidate will integrate rigorous experiments and quantitative models, consider realistic process conditions, and develop a credible path from focused separations research toward broader hydrometallurgical processes. Potential for growth into complementary areas such as leaching, precipitation, crystallization, and electrochemical recovery is valued; expertise across all these areas is not expected. The successful candidate will develop an independent, internationally recognized research program and seek funding, both individually and collaboratively, from government and industrial sources. Responsibilities will also include teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, supervising undergraduate, master’s and doctoral students, and providing service to the University and the broader community. The successful candidate will be expected to develop an equitable, diverse, and inclusive learning and research environment that is respectful of all students, staff and faculty. The appointee will have the opportunity to work within the Industrial Research Chair in Hydrometallurgy to develop research, engage industrial sponsors and sustain UBC’s excellence in hydrometallurgy. Established in 1988 with nine Canadian sponsors, the Industrial Research Chair in Hydrometallurgy at UBC now has 17 sponsors worldwide. The Chair comprises a group of professors who are world-renowned innovators and researchers in their respective fields. The Chair focuses on three key mandates: conducting research, delivering technical short courses to sponsors, and training undergraduate and graduate students.  Developing and delivering Chair short courses will be a core contribution, initially emphasizing aqueous chemistry, solvent extraction, ion exchange and flowsheet integration. Teaching contributions are also expected in undergraduate and graduate hydrometallurgy and related subjects. Experience with industrial process development or pilot-scale investigations would be valuable, assessed relative to career stage. A Ph.D. in Metallurgical Engineering, Materials Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Chemistry or a closely related discipline is required. Applicants should demonstrate a strong record of research achievement and teaching ability, or clear potential to excel in these areas, relative to career stage. The successful candidate must be registered or be eligible to register as a Professional Engineer (P.Eng.) with Engineers & Geoscientists British Columbia (EGBC). Registration is required within five years of appointment. For more information, please visit www.egbc.ca . Applications Further information on the Department is available at www.mtrl.ubc.ca and information on the Faculty of Applied Science is available at https://apsc.ubc.ca/faculty . UBC recognizes the legitimate impact that leaves (e.g., maternity leave, parental leave, leaves due to illness, leaves due to caring for family members, slowdowns due to chronic illness or disability, or COVID-19 impacts) can have on research achievement and commits to ensuring that leaves are taken into careful consideration during the assessment process. Candidates are encouraged to highlight in their application how career interruptions and personal circumstances have affected their careers. The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing. Applications must be submitted online at https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs . Applicants should submit an application package including: ·        a cover letter (maximum 2 pages*) ·        a current curriculum vitae ·        a  statement of research interests (maximum 3 pages*) ·        a statement of teaching philosophy and interests (maximum 1 page*) ·        a statement on how their research and/or teaching would contribute to an equitable, diverse and inclusive (EDI) academic environment (maximum 1 page*) ·        names and contact information (including email addresses) of four referees * Additional pages will be removed. To comply with the Government of Canada’s reporting requirements, the University gathers information about applicants’ status as either a permanent resident of Canada or Canadian Citizen. Therefore, all applications must include one of the following statements in their cover letter: I am a citizen or permanent resident of Canada; or I am not a citizen or permanent resident of Canada.   The initial closing date for applications is midnight January 4, 2027, but applications will be accepted until a suitable candidate is found. The Faculty of Applied Science is committed to offering equitable salaries, taking into consideration the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate as well as their years in rank. The expected starting salary range for the Assistant Professor position is $120,000 - $130,000 per annum. The expected starting salary range for the Associate Professor position is $165,000 - $185,000 per annum. This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits. The University of British Columbia hires on the basis on merit, assessed in areas of scholarly activity, teaching and service, and is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are accepted, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process, we will make efforts to create an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities). Confidential accommodations are available on request for all candidates taking part in all aspects of the selection process. To confidentially request such accommodations, please contact Michelle Tierney, Manager - Business Operations at michelle.tierney@ubc.ca. To learn more about UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility, visit https://hr.ubc.ca/CWA . Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. The Department is located on UBC's Point Grey campus in Vancouver, British Columbia, a vibrant and culturally diverse city situated between the Pacific Ocean and the Coast Mountains and consistently ranked among the world's most livable cities. The University of British Columbia and the Department of Materials Engineering acknowledge the hən̓q̓əmin̓əm̓-speaking xwməθkwəy̓əm (Musqueam) people on whose traditional, ancestral, and unceded territory the university resides."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Assistant-Professor-in-High-Temperature-Metals-Processing_JR26351": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in High Temperature Metals Processing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor in High Temperature Metals Processing Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Administrative Leadership | Department of Materials Engineering | Faculty of Applied Science (Daan Maijer) Job End Date June 30, 2032 Posting End Date January 3, 2027 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected starting salary range for the Assistant Professor position is $120,000 - $130,000 per annum The Department of Materials Engineering at the University of British Columbia (Vancouver campus) seeks qualified applicants for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in high-temperature metals processing (in exceptional cases a tenure-track, or tenured, appointment at the rank of Associate Professor may be considered). The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Over its long history, the Department of Materials Engineering at UBC has distinguished itself with excellence in research, teaching and service in Materials Engineering. The Department has a strong record of collaborative work within UBC and industry and promotes interdisciplinary research. Through the study of high-temperature processes and their influence on material behaviour and product quality, this appointment will strengthen connections among extractive metallurgy, physical metallurgy and manufacturing at UBC. We seek an emerging researcher with a strong foundation in heat transfer and transport phenomena, supported by expertise in several of the following: fluid flow, thermodynamics, reaction kinetics, and solidification. Relevant research areas include: continuous, shape casting and other processes involving solidification; high-temperature extraction, recycling and refining; plasma processing, furnaces and thermal treatment, and other relevant areas. Applications addressing energy efficiency, emissions reduction, resource recovery and product quality are of particular interest. The candidate will integrate physically grounded mathematical models with novel experimental approaches to identify controlling mechanisms, improve sustainability, process performance and enhance product quality. Experience designing coupled experiments/simulations, making measurements under challenging process conditions, or investigating processes at laboratory, pilot or industrial scales is valued. Applicants should demonstrate depth in a defined research area and a vision for extending their expertise across materials, processes and scales. Expertise in every area listed is not expected; diverse intellectual interests, engineering judgement and potential for growth will be important considerations. The successful candidate will develop an independent, internationally recognized research program and seek funding, both individually and collaboratively, from government and industrial sources. Responsibilities will also include teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, supervising master’s and doctoral students, and providing service to the University and the broader community. The successful candidate will be expected to develop an equitable, diverse and inclusive learning and research environment that is respectful of all students, staff and faculty. Teaching contributions are expected in thermodynamics, transport phenomena, kinetics and materials processing, with additional contributions (e.g., developing new courses) aligned with the candidate’s expertise. The appointee will be encouraged to build collaborations across departmental research areas and sustained industrial partnerships that connect fundamental research with practical process improvements. A Ph.D. in Materials Engineering, Metallurgical Engineering or a related field is required. Applicants should demonstrate a strong record of research achievement and teaching ability, or clear potential to excel in these areas, relative to career stage. The successful candidate must be registered or be eligible to register as a Professional Engineer (P.Eng.) with Engineers & Geoscientists British Columbia (EGBC). Registration is required within five years of appointment. For more information, please visit www.egbc.ca .   Applications Further information on the Department is available at www.mtrl.ubc.ca and information on the Faculty of Applied Science is available at https://apsc.ubc.ca/faculty . UBC recognizes the legitimate impact that leaves (e.g., maternity leave, parental leave, leaves due to illness, leaves due to caring for family members, slowdowns due to chronic illness or disability, or COVID-19 impacts) can have on research achievement and commits to ensuring that leaves are taken into careful consideration during the assessment process. Candidates are encouraged to highlight in their application how career interruptions and personal circumstances have affected their careers. The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing. Applications must be submitted online at https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs . Applicants should submit a letter stating their suitability for the position and include: a cover letter explaining the candidate’s interest and fit to the position. a current curriculum vitae a 1-page statement of research interests* a 1-page statement of teaching philosophy and interests* a 1-page statement on how their research and/or teaching would contribute to an equitable, diverse and inclusive (EDI) academic environment* names and contact information (including email addresses) of four referees * Additional pages will be removed. To comply with the Government of Canada’s reporting requirements, the University gathers information about applicants’ status as either a permanent resident of Canada or Canadian Citizen. Therefore, all applications must include one of the following statements in their cover letter: I am a citizen or permanent resident of Canada; or I am not a citizen or permanent resident of Canada. The initial closing date for applications is midnight January 4, 2027, but applications will be accepted until a suitable candidate is found. The Faculty of Applied Science is committed to offering equitable salaries, taking into consideration the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate as well as their years in rank. The expected starting salary range for the Assistant Professor position is $120,000 - $130,000 per annum. The expected starting salary range for the Associate Professor position is $165,000 - $185,000 per annum. This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits. The University of British Columbia hires on the basis of merit, assessed in areas of scholarly activity, teaching and service, and is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are accepted, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we will make efforts to create an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities). Confidential accommodations are available on request for all candidates taking part in all aspects of the selection process. To confidentially request such accommodations, please contact Michelle Tierney, Manager - Business Operations at michelle.tierney@ubc.ca To learn more about UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility, visit https://hr.ubc.ca/CWA . Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. The Department is located on UBC's Point Grey campus in Vancouver, British Columbia, a vibrant and culturally diverse city situated between the Pacific Ocean and the Coast Mountains and consistently ranked among the world's most livable cities. The University of British Columbia and the Department of Materials Engineering acknowledge the hən̓q̓əmin̓əm̓-speaking xwməθkwəy̓əm (Musqueam) people on whose traditional, ancestral, and unceded territory the university resides."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Assistant-Professor-Research-Search--up-to-2-positions----Computer-Science_JR26478": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Research Search (up to 2 positions) - Computer Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nJob Title Assistant Professor Research Search (up to 2 positions) - Computer Science Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Faculty | Tenure and Tenure Stream | Department of Computer Science | Faculty of Science (Joanna McGrenere) Job End Date Ongoing Posting End Date December 5, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Salary is expected to be within the range of $180,000 - $230,000. Faculty Position – Department of Computer Science in the Faculty of Science at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver.   The Department of Computer Science at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver invites applications for up to two full-time tenure-track positions at the rank of Assistant Professor. The anticipated  start date is July 1, 2027 . Salary is expected to be within the range of $180,000 - $230,000. The Department is interested in applicants in the areas of systems (e.g., operating systems, computer architecture, networking, embedded systems, distributed systems, and systems security), data management (e.g., databases, data systems, and DB-AI integration), and data mining.   Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a related field and must demonstrate evidence of research success and a high potential to be leaders in their research field. The successful candidate should have a strong record of research productivity commensurate with their experience and will be expected to develop an independent research program in an area that complements existing Department expertise and aligns with opportunities at UBC. The successful candidate will be expected to effectively supervise Computer Science graduate students, collaborate with other faculty members, obtain external funding, teach undergraduate and graduate Computer Science courses, and actively participate in departmental activities. They will have a strong commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion, to create a welcoming community for all, particularly those who are historically, persistently or systemically marginalized.   UBC is ranked as a top CS program in Canada and in the top 30 Computer Science departments worldwide, with 3,000 undergrads, over 280 grads, and 71 faculty including 22 new hires in the past six years. https://www.cs.ubc.ca/ How to Apply   Applications must be submitted through Academic Jobs Online and include the following: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae including a list of publications. Research statement. Teaching statement describing your teaching interests and record of your teaching experience. The names of three referees who have been asked to upload their reference letters to your application. Diversity statement (1 page) describing your lived background experience (if comfortable), and your past experience and future plans regarding working with a diverse student body, and contributing to a culture of equity and inclusion. The closing date for applications is December 7, 2026. At the discretion of the committee the deadline may be extended if the number of applications received by that date is deemed insufficient. All applications which are complete by the final deadline, including the receipt of at least three reference letters, are guaranteed full consideration.   Questions about the search can be directed to the committee chair, Dr. Ivan Beschastnikh, at the address  research-recruiting-open-chair@cs.ubc.ca   Additional Information   UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence.  An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged.  Inclusion is built by individual and institutional responsibility through continuous engagement with diversity to inspire people, ideas, and actions for a better world. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Métis, Inuk, or Indigenous person. UBC acknowledges that certain circumstances may cause career interruptions that legitimately affect an applicant’s record of research or educational leadership achievement. We encourage applicants to note in their applications whether they would like consideration given to the impact of any circumstances, such as those due to health or family reasons, in order to allow for a fair assessment of their research productivity.   All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.   This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits   The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing.   If you have any needs or questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the job application, recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Center For Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/working-injury-illness-or-disability/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca .   The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible work environment for all members of its workforce. Within this hiring process we will make efforts to create an accessible process for all candidates (including but not limited to disabled people). Confidential accommodations are available on request by contacting Jessica McKay at recruit-admin@cs.ubc.ca .   Applicants will be asked to complete an equity survey when submitting their application through Academic Jobs Online.  The survey information will not be used to determine eligibility for employment, but will be collated to provide data that can assist us in understanding the diversity of our applicant pool.  Applicants’ participation in the survey is voluntary and confidential.   UBC’s campuses are located on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territories of the Syilx (Okanagan) Peoples and of the Coast Salish Peoples, including the territories of the xwməθkwəy̓əm (Musqueam), Skwxwú7mesh (Squamish), and Stó:lō and Səl̓ílwətaʔ/Selilwitulh (Tsleil Waututh) Nations."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Vancouver-Campus---Vancouver-BC-Canada/Associate-Professor-with-Tenure-Research-Search---Computer-Science_JR26475": {
      "title": "Associate Professor with Tenure Research Search - Computer Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nJob Title Associate Professor with Tenure Research Search - Computer Science Job Category Faculty Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Faculty | Tenure and Tenure Stream | Department of Computer Science | Faculty of Science (Joanna McGrenere) Job End Date Ongoing Posting End Date December 5, 2026 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. Salary is expected to be within the range of $200,000 - $250,000. Faculty Position – Department of Computer Science in the Faculty of Science at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver.   The Department of Computer Science at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver is seeking an outstanding investigator for a full-time tenured position at the rank of Associate Professor. The anticipated  start date is July 1, 2027 . Salary is expected to be within the range of $200,000 - $250,000. The Department is interested in applicants in the areas of systems (e.g., operating systems, computer architecture, networking, embedded systems, distributed systems, and systems security), data management (e.g., databases, data systems, and DB-AI integration), and data mining. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a related field and a strong, sustained record of research excellence, including significant scholarly contributions and demonstrated impact in their research area. The successful candidate will have an established, internationally recognized independent research program in an area that complements existing Department expertise and aligns with opportunities at UBC. They will have demonstrated research leadership, success in supervising and mentoring Computer Science graduate students, productive collaborations with other researchers, and success in securing external research funding. They will also have a strong record of effective teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels and sustained contributions to departmental and broader academic activities. They will have a strong commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion, and to fostering a welcoming community for all, particularly those who are historically, persistently or systemically marginalized.   UBC is ranked as a top CS program in Canada and in the top 30 Computer Science departments worldwide, with 3,000 undergrads, over 280 grads, and 71 faculty including 22 new hires in the past six years. https://www.cs.ubc.ca/ How to Apply   Applications must be submitted through Academic Jobs Onlin e and include the following: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae including a list of publications. Research statement. Teaching statement describing your teaching interests and record of your teaching experience. The names of three referees who have been asked to upload their reference letters to your application. Diversity statement (1 page) describing your lived background experience (if comfortable), and your past experience and future plans regarding working with a diverse student body, and contributing to a culture of equity and inclusion. The closing date for applications is December 7, 2026. At the discretion of the committee the deadline may be extended if the number of applications received by that date is deemed insufficient. All applications which are complete by the final deadline, including the receipt of at least three reference letters, are guaranteed full consideration.   Questions about the search can be directed to the committee chair, Dr. Ivan Beschastnikh, at the address  research-recruiting-open-chair@cs.ubc.ca   Additional Information   UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence.  An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged.  Inclusion is built by individual and institutional responsibility through continuous engagement with diversity to inspire people, ideas, and actions for a better world. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Métis, Inuk, or Indigenous person. UBC acknowledges that certain circumstances may cause career interruptions that legitimately affect an applicant’s record of research or educational leadership achievement. We encourage applicants to note in their applications whether they would like consideration given to the impact of any circumstances, such as those due to health or family reasons, in order to allow for a fair assessment of their research productivity.   All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority.   This position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package. For details, see https://hr.ubc.ca/benefits   The University of British Columbia's primary language of instruction and research is English. Accordingly, individuals are required to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing.   If you have any needs or questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the job application, recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Center For Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/working-injury-illness-or-disability/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca .   The University is committed to creating and maintaining an accessible work environment for all members of its workforce. Within this hiring process we will make efforts to create an accessible process for all candidates (including but not limited to disabled people). Confidential accommodations are available on request by contacting Jessica McKay at recruit-admin@cs.ubc.ca .   Applicants will be asked to complete an equity survey when submitting their application through Academic Jobs Online.  The survey information will not be used to determine eligibility for employment, but will be collated to provide data that can assist us in understanding the diversity of our applicant pool.  Applicants’ participation in the survey is voluntary and confidential.   UBC’s campuses are located on the traditional, ancestral, and unceded territories of the Syilx (Okanagan) Peoples and of the Coast Salish Peoples, including the territories of the xwməθkwəy̓əm (Musqueam), Skwxwú7mesh (Squamish), and Stó:lō and Səl̓ílwətaʔ/Selilwitulh (Tsleil Waututh) Nations."
    },
    "https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs/job/UBC-Alternate-Site---Victoria-BC-Canada/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_JR26465": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nJob Title Postdoctoral Research Fellow Job Category Faculty Non Bargaining Job Family Group Academic Department Therapeutics Initiative | Department of Anesthesiology, Pharmacology and Therapeutics | Faculty of Medicine (Colin Dormuth) Job End Date December 31, 2027 Posting End Date November 28, 2027 Note: Applications will be accepted until 11:59 PM on the Posting End Date. The expected pay range for this position is $68,000 - $72,000 per annum. At UBC, we believe that attracting and sustaining a diverse workforce is key to the successful pursuit of excellence in research, innovation, and learning for all faculty, staff and students. Our commitment to employment equity helps achieve inclusion and fairness, brings rich diversity to UBC as a workplace, and creates the necessary conditions for a rewarding career. ​​​​​​ Dr. Colin Dormuth, Department of Anesthesiology, Pharmacology, and Therapeutics, is seeking a full-time (1.0 FTE) Postdoctoral Research Fellow to conduct research using large population-based databases to assess the safety and effectiveness of medications in a real-world setting, and support other provincially and nationally funded research projects, including the Canadian Network for Observational Drug Effect Studies (CNODES, https://www.cnodes.ca). The fellow will work from the Therapeutics Initiative offices in downtown Victoria, BC (preferred) or UBC Point Grey Campus in Vancouver, BC. The UBC Department of Anesthesiology, Pharmacology, and Therapeutics is an amalgamation of three important and inter-related areas of medicine: anesthesiology, pharmacology, and therapeutics. Brought together by common interests, we challenge ourselves to create opportunities to share each other’s expertise in order to foster excellence in research, education and knowledge translation.   RESPONSIBILITIES Reporting to Dr. Colin Dormuth, the incumbent will be responsible for:   Conducting substantive, methodologically rigorous pharmacoepidemiologic research analyses using large healthcare administrative databases; Writing and executing SAS code to support projects and Ministry of Health deliverables; Participating in study design, quality assurance testing, documenting coding methods, and interpreting results; Contributing to literature reviews and syntheses; Writing research abstracts, summary reports, and scientific articles, and submitting articles to peer-reviewed journals; Presenting study results to the scientific community at national and international conferences; Identifying and pursuing secondary research projects and research funding; and Collaborating with the other research teams.   QUALIFICATIONS Successful applicants will have: Doctorate degree in Epidemiology, Biostatistics, or Economics Minimum of two years related experience Experience in pharmacoepidemiology is an asset Demonstrated skills in data management and programming using statistical packages (SAS, R and SQL and occasionally STATA); Demonstrated knowledge of research processes, systematic reviews, and syntheses; Education/training and experience in quantitative research methods, and multivariate statistical analysis; Excellent communication skills, including strong writing, research, and interpersonal skills; High-level conceptual, analytical, and critical appraisal skills; and Demonstrated initiative and willingness to work closely with members of a research team. Willingness to respect diverse perspectives, including perspectives in conflict with one’s own. Demonstrates a commitment to enhancing one’s own awareness, knowledge, and skills related to equity, diversity, and inclusion.   The expected salary for this position is $68,000 - $72,000 per annum. The Faculty of Medicine is committed to offering equitable and competitive salaries, commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. APPLICATION PROCEDURE An application package should include: A cover letter, noting research experience and interest in the position; A detailed curriculum vitae with all relevant employment and academic experience; 1-2 writing samples (publications) or thesis chapter; Contact information for three referees   Applications should be submitted online at https://ubc.wd10.myworkdayjobs.com/ubcfacultyjobs Should you have any queries around this position, please contact Patrick Salame at patrick.salame@ubc.ca .   Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date for this position is January 1, 2027 or upon a date to be mutually agreed.   We invite applications from qualified candidates who share our commitment to employment equity and inclusive excellence, and we welcome applications from candidates belonging to historically, persistently, or systemically marginalized groups: Indigenous (First Nation, Métis, Inuit) Peoples, racialized persons, persons with disabilities, women, 2SLGBTQIA+ people, and trans and non-binary people. The University is committed to creating and maintaining an inclusive and equitable work environment for all members of its workforce. An inclusive work environment presumes an environment where differences are appreciated, recognized, and integrated into current structures, planning, and decision-making modes. Within this hiring process we are committed to creating an inclusive and equitable process for all candidates (including but not limited to people with disabilities).  Confidential accommodations are available on request. Please contact the Assistant Manager, HR & Administration via email at anae-hr.admin@ubc.ca. If you have any questions regarding accommodations or accessibility during the recruitment and hiring process or for more information and support, please visit UBC’s Centre for Workplace Accessibility website at https://hr.ubc.ca/health-and-wellbeing/workplace-accessibility/centre-workplace-accessibility or contact the Centre at workplace.accessibility@ubc.ca .    With gratitude, we acknowledge that the University of British Columbia Faculty of Medicine and its distributed programs, which include four university academic campuses, are located on traditional, ancestral and unceded territories of First Nations Peoples and communities around the province. Our Vision: To Transform Health for Everyone.   Ranked among the world’s top medical schools with the fifth-largest MD enrollment in North America, the UBC Faculty of Medicine is a leader in both the science and the practice of medicine. Across British Columbia, more than 12,000 faculty and staff are training the next generation of doctors and health care professionals, making remarkable discoveries, and helping to create the pathways to better health for our communities at home and around the world.   The Faculty - comprised of approximately 2,200 administrative support, technical/research and management and professional staff, as well approximately 650 full-time academic and over 10,000 clinical faculty members - is composed of 19 academic basic science and/or clinical departments, three schools, and 24 research centres and institutes. Together with its University and Health Authority partners, the Faculty delivers innovative programs and conducts research in the areas of health and life sciences. Faculty, staff and trainees are located at university campuses, clinical academic campuses in hospital settings and other regionally based centres across the province. UBC - One of the World's Leading Universities. As one of the world's leading universities, the University of British Columbia creates an exceptional learning environment that fosters global citizenship, advances a civil and sustainable society, and supports outstanding research to serve the people of British Columbia, Canada and the world. UBC hires on the basis of merit and is committed to employment equity. All qualified persons are encouraged to apply. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. An open and diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been underrepresented or discouraged. We encourage applications from members of groups that have been marginalized on any grounds enumerated under the B.C. Human Rights Code, including sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, racialization, disability, political belief, religion, marital or family status, age, and/or status as a First Nation, Metis, Inuit, or Indigenous person. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority."
    }
  },
